1
00:00:14,014 --> 00:00:17,817
A makeover project
for your most unhinged leftovers.

2
00:00:17,884 --> 00:00:21,354
The best chefs in Korea will
turn your fridge inside out.

3
00:00:21,421 --> 00:00:23,490
The classy cooking show has returned.

4
00:00:23,556 --> 00:00:26,926
<i>-Chef & My Fridge!</i>
<i>-My Fridge!</i>

5
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:29,062
It's great to see everyone.

6
00:00:29,129 --> 00:00:30,897
IT'S GREAT TO SEE YOU

7
00:00:30,964 --> 00:00:33,833
And we're seeing another familiar face.

8
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:35,635
Edward Lee is here with us today.

9
00:00:35,702 --> 00:00:36,770
Have you been well?

10
00:00:36,836 --> 00:00:38,171
Yes, I've been busy.

11
00:00:38,238 --> 00:00:39,773
I've been traveling in the US,

12
00:00:39,839 --> 00:00:43,209
as well as Taiwan and Hong Kong.

13
00:00:43,276 --> 00:00:44,444
Even Taiwan and Hong Kong?

14
00:00:45,311 --> 00:00:46,379
He's still as busy as ever.

15
00:00:46,446 --> 00:00:48,748
-It's no longer Gordon Ramsay.
-No.

16
00:00:48,815 --> 00:00:51,584
-It's now Edward Lee's era.
-Of course.

17
00:00:51,651 --> 00:00:52,886
SHAKING HEAD

18
00:00:52,952 --> 00:00:54,988
-Your friend is missing.
-That's right.

19
00:00:55,055 --> 00:00:56,956
-Your friend, Hyun-suk.
-Where's Grandpa?

20
00:00:57,023 --> 00:00:58,291
-What?
-Where is he?

21
00:00:58,358 --> 00:01:00,460
San Francisco, USA.

22
00:01:00,693 --> 00:01:01,828
He went to throw a first pitch.

23
00:01:01,895 --> 00:01:02,829
What?

24
00:01:02,896 --> 00:01:04,631
-The first pitch?
-Not the KBO?

25
00:01:04,697 --> 00:01:08,068
-In America?
-I heard he had to pay for his own flight.

26
00:01:08,234 --> 00:01:09,669
ONLY HIS SPECULATION

27
00:01:09,736 --> 00:01:11,638
He got invited,
but he had to get there himself.

28
00:01:11,704 --> 00:01:13,640
Some even say he paid to pitch.

29
00:01:13,706 --> 00:01:15,308
I saw a video of him.

30
00:01:15,375 --> 00:01:18,745
When you do the ceremonial pitch,
there's a batter who swings, right?

31
00:01:18,812 --> 00:01:20,580
But I didn't see one there.

32
00:01:20,647 --> 00:01:23,550
-It was early this morning, Korea time.
-Today?

33
00:01:23,616 --> 00:01:26,086
I heard it didn't go well.

34
00:01:26,152 --> 00:01:27,153
-Why not?
-Why not?

35
00:01:27,220 --> 00:01:28,755
His team lost.

36
00:01:28,822 --> 00:01:30,423
The unlucky charm.

37
00:01:30,490 --> 00:01:32,892
-He was an unlucky charm.
-He should've brought them luck.

38
00:01:32,959 --> 00:01:35,428
-An international unlucky charm.
-He should've made them win.

39
00:01:35,495 --> 00:01:37,664
ATTACKED EVEN WHEN ABSENT…

40
00:01:37,730 --> 00:01:40,300
He should've supported Lee Jung-hoo.

41
00:01:40,366 --> 00:01:42,469
Jung-hoo, if your schedule allows it,

42
00:01:42,535 --> 00:01:44,838
-we'd love to treat you to a meal.
-That'd be great.

43
00:01:44,904 --> 00:01:46,272
-Please come by.
-Sounds great.

44
00:01:46,339 --> 00:01:49,142
-Good luck, Jung-hoo.
-Good luck.

45
00:01:49,209 --> 00:01:51,244
Hyun-suk, please return safely
on your own.

46
00:01:51,311 --> 00:01:52,645
"On your own"?

47
00:01:52,712 --> 00:01:56,416
Let's find out who today's guests are.

48
00:01:56,483 --> 00:01:58,184
Please welcome the fridge owners.

49
00:01:58,251 --> 00:01:59,819
Come on out!

50
00:02:00,787 --> 00:02:02,355
Today's fridge owners.

51
00:02:02,422 --> 00:02:03,756
You'll be happy to see them.

52
00:02:03,823 --> 00:02:05,892
Please welcome

53
00:02:05,959 --> 00:02:09,729
-Lee Seon-min and CODE KUNST!
-Hello.

54
00:02:10,430 --> 00:02:11,498
WELCOME

55
00:02:11,564 --> 00:02:13,666
-Nice to meet you. Hello.
-Hello.

56
00:02:13,733 --> 00:02:16,536
-Whoa.
-It's great to see you two.

57
00:02:16,603 --> 00:02:18,571
-Make yourself comfortable.
-The set is huge.

58
00:02:18,638 --> 00:02:20,173
This man right next to me

59
00:02:21,307 --> 00:02:23,610
<i>has taken over</i>
<i>the world of online variety shows,</i>

60
00:02:23,676 --> 00:02:27,981
<i>and he's been making his way</i>
<i>to network TV shows as well.</i>

61
00:02:28,047 --> 00:02:30,116
He's the hottest potato at the moment.

62
00:02:30,183 --> 00:02:31,351
Please welcome Lee Seon-min!

63
00:02:31,417 --> 00:02:32,385
Hello.

64
00:02:32,452 --> 00:02:34,354
-I'm such a huge fan.
-Hello.

65
00:02:34,420 --> 00:02:36,589
-Please say hello to the viewers too.
-The hottest right now.

66
00:02:36,656 --> 00:02:38,458
Not the biggest trend
or even the mid-trend,

67
00:02:38,525 --> 00:02:40,160
but the microtrending Lee Seon-min.

68
00:02:40,226 --> 00:02:41,294
Right.

69
00:02:41,361 --> 00:02:44,063
-He has a strong voice.
-He does.

70
00:02:44,130 --> 00:02:45,532
All right. The next guy.

71
00:02:45,598 --> 00:02:47,800
A genius music producer by profession,

72
00:02:47,867 --> 00:02:49,769
<i>and a TV personality on the side,</i>

73
00:02:49,836 --> 00:02:50,803
<i>CODE KUNST!</i>

74
00:02:50,870 --> 00:02:53,373
-Welcome to the show.
-Hello.

75
00:02:53,439 --> 00:02:55,775
-COKUN.
-I'm the guy who makes music

76
00:02:55,842 --> 00:02:56,976
and lives alone.

77
00:02:57,043 --> 00:03:00,813
-But today, I'm COKUN that's here to eat.
-Of course.

78
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:03,316
-I never thought I'd see you here.
-I agree.

79
00:03:03,383 --> 00:03:05,718
-I'm really nervous right now.
-You are?

80
00:03:05,785 --> 00:03:06,686
I always thought

81
00:03:07,487 --> 00:03:09,989
I did not belong on this show.

82
00:03:10,056 --> 00:03:11,891
A PLACE I SHOULD NOT BE?

83
00:03:11,958 --> 00:03:14,494
-He's known as a light eater.
-He is.

84
00:03:14,561 --> 00:03:17,130
But Joo Woo-jae
has already started that track,

85
00:03:17,197 --> 00:03:18,698
-so anyone can come now.
-Really?

86
00:03:18,765 --> 00:03:21,367
I was rather encouraged
after seeing his fridge.

87
00:03:21,634 --> 00:03:24,170
HIS FRIDGE GAVE SOMEONE COURAGE

88
00:03:24,237 --> 00:03:25,305
Mine will be

89
00:03:25,371 --> 00:03:26,673
better than what you expect.

90
00:03:26,739 --> 00:03:28,374
-Your episode will do better?
-No.

91
00:03:28,441 --> 00:03:29,709
I mean my fridge.

92
00:03:29,776 --> 00:03:31,444
-His fridge.
-His fridge.

93
00:03:31,511 --> 00:03:33,046
CONFIDENT ABOUT HIS FRIDGE

94
00:03:33,112 --> 00:03:35,982
-I mean, that's what I think.
-He's telling the chefs not to worry.

95
00:03:36,049 --> 00:03:40,019
I heard you were very excited
after we invited you to the show.

96
00:03:40,086 --> 00:03:41,187
I really was.

97
00:03:41,254 --> 00:03:42,555
When my manager

98
00:03:42,622 --> 00:03:45,525
gets an offer from big shows,
he tells me about it like this.

99
00:03:45,592 --> 00:03:47,860
"Seon-min, we got a delicious one."

100
00:03:47,927 --> 00:03:50,063
-He calls it "delicious."
-A delicious show.

101
00:03:50,129 --> 00:03:51,965
-Instead of just saying the title?
-This time, he said,

102
00:03:52,031 --> 00:03:54,133
"We got a delicious one,
and I mean that literally."

103
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:55,034
I asked, "What is it?"

104
00:03:55,101 --> 00:03:57,270
He said, "<i>Chef & My Fridge.</i>"

105
00:03:57,337 --> 00:04:00,139
I was so excited to hear it.

106
00:04:00,206 --> 00:04:02,675
My work is mostly online,
so I saw a comment like this one.

107
00:04:02,742 --> 00:04:06,479
"You've recently been invited
to many shows on legacy media."

108
00:04:06,546 --> 00:04:10,250
"The only show left for you to do
is <i>Chef & My Fridge.</i>"

109
00:04:10,316 --> 00:04:13,152
-I saw that comment.
-The final one.

110
00:04:13,219 --> 00:04:14,854
-The destination.
-Yes.

111
00:04:14,921 --> 00:04:17,657
It felt like checking off
an item off my list,

112
00:04:17,724 --> 00:04:19,392
so I'm very moved right now.

113
00:04:19,459 --> 00:04:22,095
We're also streamed abroad
through a platform.

114
00:04:22,295 --> 00:04:23,129
LET'S GO GLOBAL

115
00:04:23,196 --> 00:04:24,864
Seon-min was first recruited

116
00:04:24,931 --> 00:04:28,434
through a comedian audition
held by S TV network in 2016,

117
00:04:28,501 --> 00:04:31,271
<i>but their sketch comedy show</i>
<i>got canceled the next year.</i>

118
00:04:31,337 --> 00:04:35,908
But he stayed strong through a decade
and is living his glory days right now.

119
00:04:35,975 --> 00:04:38,711
He's a regular cast member
on ten different channels.

120
00:04:38,778 --> 00:04:41,314
-What?
-He's a regular on ten channels?

121
00:04:41,381 --> 00:04:44,951
The brand reputation index
released its analysis in September,

122
00:04:45,018 --> 00:04:47,186
and he came in second,

123
00:04:47,253 --> 00:04:49,489
right after Yu Jae-seok.

124
00:04:49,555 --> 00:04:51,457
-You can't beat Jae-seok.
-That's not easy.

125
00:04:51,524 --> 00:04:53,960
-He's practically first place.
-Practically.

126
00:04:54,027 --> 00:04:56,329
I'm flattered.

127
00:04:56,396 --> 00:04:58,765
I've been a fan for a long time,

128
00:04:58,831 --> 00:05:02,568
since the time when you
impersonated Noh Joo-hyun.

129
00:05:02,635 --> 00:05:04,804
Hello, I am Noh Joo-yeon.

130
00:05:11,878 --> 00:05:14,147
I've been a fan since then.

131
00:05:14,213 --> 00:05:16,883
-Thank you.
-It feels like I'm seeing a celebrity.

132
00:05:16,949 --> 00:05:19,218
-It feels like that.
-Me too, sir.

133
00:05:19,285 --> 00:05:20,720
-I feel like…
-Am I older than you?

134
00:05:20,787 --> 00:05:23,056
I believe you're quite
a bit older than I am.

135
00:05:23,122 --> 00:05:24,223
"Quite a bit older"?

136
00:05:24,290 --> 00:05:25,325
When were you born?

137
00:05:25,391 --> 00:05:26,759
I was born in 1988.

138
00:05:27,126 --> 00:05:28,428
BORN IN 1978 GETS FLUSTERED

139
00:05:28,494 --> 00:05:29,595
The year of the Olympics.

140
00:05:29,662 --> 00:05:31,331
-You're 12 years younger than me?
-Really?

141
00:05:31,397 --> 00:05:33,366
-I'm sorry, sir.
-He's G-DRAGON and TAEYANG's peer.

142
00:05:33,433 --> 00:05:35,902
-He's only two years older than you.
-Only two years.

143
00:05:35,968 --> 00:05:37,470
Chef Edward thought

144
00:05:37,537 --> 00:05:39,639
I was his age.

145
00:05:39,706 --> 00:05:40,873
I'm sorry.

146
00:05:40,940 --> 00:05:43,343
BORN IN 1972, BORN IN 1988

147
00:05:43,409 --> 00:05:45,278
That's fascinating.

148
00:05:45,345 --> 00:05:49,816
I feel like I'm living life
at 1.7 times the speed compared to others.

149
00:05:49,882 --> 00:05:51,751
I got this often when I was younger.

150
00:05:51,818 --> 00:05:53,353
"People like you will

151
00:05:53,419 --> 00:05:56,489
-look much younger as you age."
-Yes.

152
00:05:56,556 --> 00:05:59,392
But I've been proving that saying wrong.

153
00:05:59,959 --> 00:06:01,928
-Is that so?
-I continue to age

154
00:06:01,994 --> 00:06:04,364
at double speed compared to the others.

155
00:06:04,430 --> 00:06:06,366
The law of aging acceleration.

156
00:06:06,432 --> 00:06:08,267
"The law of aging acceleration"?

157
00:06:08,334 --> 00:06:09,969
-Back in seventh grade.
-Seventh grade?

158
00:06:10,036 --> 00:06:11,938
When I was only 13 years old,

159
00:06:12,004 --> 00:06:14,841
I got on a bus,
and I don't remember how much it was.

160
00:06:14,907 --> 00:06:17,110
But I paid 1,000 won,
and the driver put me down

161
00:06:17,176 --> 00:06:18,778
for the adult fare.

162
00:06:18,845 --> 00:06:20,546
FACEPALM

163
00:06:20,613 --> 00:06:22,348
I said, "I'm a teenager."

164
00:06:22,415 --> 00:06:23,649
He stared at me for a while,

165
00:06:24,417 --> 00:06:26,152
then said, "Don't lie, young man."

166
00:06:26,219 --> 00:06:28,121
"Young man"?

167
00:06:28,187 --> 00:06:31,457
-But he was only 13.
-"Young man."

168
00:06:31,524 --> 00:06:33,493
But now, when I look at
my picture from back then…

169
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:36,662
-Do you get why?
-I get why he was like that.

170
00:06:36,729 --> 00:06:39,632
I look like my dad's peer.

171
00:06:39,699 --> 00:06:43,002
Seon-min's most famous nickname

172
00:06:43,069 --> 00:06:46,172
-is "Married Man Energy."
-Yes, right.

173
00:06:46,239 --> 00:06:50,243
Chef Eun-yeong thought
he was already married.

174
00:06:50,309 --> 00:06:55,281
I thought he'd even have
a son and a daughter,

175
00:06:55,348 --> 00:06:57,583
but I found out that he lives alone.

176
00:06:57,650 --> 00:06:59,318
I'm sorry to say this,

177
00:06:59,385 --> 00:07:01,320
but I wondered
if you had done it and returned.

178
00:07:01,387 --> 00:07:03,189
"Done" what?

179
00:07:03,256 --> 00:07:04,123
Getting married once.

180
00:07:04,190 --> 00:07:06,993
Chef Nam-no is laughing too hard.

181
00:07:07,059 --> 00:07:08,928
-That can happen.
-Well, that's…

182
00:07:08,995 --> 00:07:12,532
People have been saying
I look like a dad since a decade ago.

183
00:07:12,598 --> 00:07:15,501
-I totally understand.
-Seon-min also gives off

184
00:07:15,568 --> 00:07:16,903
a few more different energies.

185
00:07:16,969 --> 00:07:18,204
-There are more?
-What?

186
00:07:18,271 --> 00:07:20,773
-VCU detective energy.
-Right.

187
00:07:20,840 --> 00:07:21,908
-Do you see it?
-I do.

188
00:07:21,974 --> 00:07:23,409
A righteous one.

189
00:07:23,476 --> 00:07:24,877
Construction site manager energy.

190
00:07:24,944 --> 00:07:27,380
-Construction manager?
-The on-site manager.

191
00:07:27,447 --> 00:07:29,015
Wearing a hard hat.

192
00:07:29,081 --> 00:07:32,385
-Admin and supply NCO energy.
-Admin and supply NCO?

193
00:07:32,452 --> 00:07:34,287
THEY ALL WORK SO WELL

194
00:07:34,353 --> 00:07:35,521
This is a good one.

195
00:07:35,588 --> 00:07:37,790
C van owner energy.

196
00:07:37,857 --> 00:07:39,158
The van owner.

197
00:07:39,225 --> 00:07:42,228
-What's that? The car?
-The car. The C van.

198
00:07:42,295 --> 00:07:44,330
And the van must be white.

199
00:07:45,097 --> 00:07:48,067
THE ENERGY OF A GUY
WHO'D GET OUT WITH A STROLLER

200
00:07:48,134 --> 00:07:49,502
And there's main chef energy too.

201
00:07:49,569 --> 00:07:51,204
-Yes.
-Main chef energy?

202
00:07:51,270 --> 00:07:52,839
-At a Chinese place.
-Do this.

203
00:07:52,905 --> 00:07:55,575
-A Chinese chef?
-Someone in Chinese cuisine.

204
00:07:55,641 --> 00:07:57,677
-At a hotel.
-Yes, at a hotel.

205
00:07:58,077 --> 00:07:59,745
LIKE MASTER LU CHINGLAI

206
00:07:59,812 --> 00:08:02,281
Even Edward from America sees that energy.

207
00:08:02,548 --> 00:08:04,817
SHAKING HANDS LIKE HE'S A FELLOW CHEF

208
00:08:05,585 --> 00:08:07,653
When I take a good look at you,

209
00:08:07,720 --> 00:08:09,922
-I can see your double eyelids…
-It's not just that.

210
00:08:09,989 --> 00:08:12,091
-His eye color.
-Yes, the color.

211
00:08:12,158 --> 00:08:13,659
-Like the sea.
-It's rather blue.

212
00:08:13,726 --> 00:08:16,095
-I was born like this.
-That's your eye color?

213
00:08:16,162 --> 00:08:19,265
Just by your eyes, don't people say
you look like Seo Kang-jun?

214
00:08:19,332 --> 00:08:20,366
Oh.

215
00:08:20,433 --> 00:08:21,501
SENSES POTENTIAL CANCELLATION

216
00:08:21,567 --> 00:08:23,102
I should be careful with my words.

217
00:08:24,737 --> 00:08:26,973
My fans have said that it's only the eyes,

218
00:08:27,039 --> 00:08:30,142
that only my eyes
have a resemblance with Seo Kang-jun.

219
00:08:30,209 --> 00:08:32,044
-Is there anyone else?
-I have a few.

220
00:08:32,111 --> 00:08:35,248
You can see Cho Hye-lyeon in me.

221
00:08:35,414 --> 00:08:37,149
HE LOOKS LIKE HER

222
00:08:37,216 --> 00:08:40,453
-And there's Moon Hee-jun.
-I can see him too.

223
00:08:40,520 --> 00:08:44,156
-I've also gotten actor Jung Man-sik.
-Jung Man-sik.

224
00:08:44,223 --> 00:08:47,560
-He has a million faces.
-I look like a lot of people.

225
00:08:47,627 --> 00:08:52,164
One of our chefs
have kept a close eye on Seon-min.

226
00:08:52,231 --> 00:08:53,566
It's Chef Jong-won.

227
00:08:53,633 --> 00:08:55,501
-What?
-On Seon-min.

228
00:08:55,568 --> 00:08:59,672
I'm a fan, so he has overtaken
my short-form algorithm.

229
00:08:59,739 --> 00:09:03,409
-My favorite was Hwang Deuk-geun.
-Yes.

230
00:09:03,476 --> 00:09:05,878
I'm Hwang Deuk-geun,
the last Mister Korea of the 20th century.

231
00:09:05,945 --> 00:09:08,848
It's a character that I play.
He's a gym owner.

232
00:09:08,915 --> 00:09:11,717
I loved seeing him shadowbox
and make poses

233
00:09:11,784 --> 00:09:13,753
like "the unbalanced arms."

234
00:09:13,819 --> 00:09:15,388
Yes, his signature pose.

235
00:09:15,454 --> 00:09:17,790
-Do you want to see it?
-Sure.

236
00:09:18,124 --> 00:09:19,158
ALWAYS READY TO ENTERTAIN

237
00:09:19,225 --> 00:09:21,827
This is the basic pose, right?

238
00:09:21,894 --> 00:09:24,163
-Unbalanced arms.
-Unbalanced arms.

239
00:09:24,230 --> 00:09:25,565
Unbalanced arms.

240
00:09:26,132 --> 00:09:27,567
THEY LOOK UNBALANCED

241
00:09:27,633 --> 00:09:30,736
I make my arms look unbalanced

242
00:09:30,803 --> 00:09:33,940
as well as my legs.
He must've enjoyed seeing this.

243
00:09:34,006 --> 00:09:35,441
-And the shadowboxing.
-Shadowboxing.

244
00:09:35,508 --> 00:09:36,676
I get to see this?

245
00:09:36,742 --> 00:09:38,010
The sketch goes like this.

246
00:09:38,077 --> 00:09:40,913
You don't need equipment. Just do it.

247
00:09:40,980 --> 00:09:42,715
And I begin to shadowbox.

248
00:09:43,950 --> 00:09:46,519
SWOOSH

249
00:09:47,320 --> 00:09:48,988
CAN'T EVEN TELL HOW MANY HE'S FIGHTING

250
00:09:49,722 --> 00:09:51,991
LOVE TO SEE IT IN REAL LIFE

251
00:09:52,058 --> 00:09:53,893
He must've seen that.

252
00:09:54,160 --> 00:09:55,962
AMAZED

253
00:09:56,028 --> 00:09:58,464
Sung-jun, did you also know him well?

254
00:09:58,531 --> 00:10:00,933
I recently saw him in the viral video

255
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,869
where he taught Woni of Rescene
how to drive.

256
00:10:03,936 --> 00:10:05,504
It was really big.

257
00:10:05,938 --> 00:10:09,542
LEE SEON-MIN & WONI'S DRIVER'S ED VIDEO

258
00:10:10,242 --> 00:10:13,112
THEY'RE NOW BOTH DOING THEIR BEST
IN THEIR INDUSTRIES

259
00:10:13,179 --> 00:10:15,648
I heard he's been receiving
the nation's support,

260
00:10:15,715 --> 00:10:18,784
so I began to watch him more since then.

261
00:10:18,851 --> 00:10:21,621
But there's a bit of controversy.

262
00:10:21,687 --> 00:10:26,892
Did Lee Seon-min help Woni get her break?

263
00:10:26,959 --> 00:10:29,462
Or was it Woni
who helped him get his break?

264
00:10:29,528 --> 00:10:31,063
-Which is it?
-He's on a tightrope.

265
00:10:31,931 --> 00:10:34,834
HOW WILL HE ESCAPE?

266
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:38,037
There are lots of traps here,
so good luck.

267
00:10:38,104 --> 00:10:40,106
-This is a rough one.
-There are lots of traps.

268
00:10:40,172 --> 00:10:41,574
This is a tough one.

269
00:10:41,641 --> 00:10:44,110
So far,
it's the toughest quiz of the year.

270
00:10:44,176 --> 00:10:45,144
It's a controversy.

271
00:10:45,211 --> 00:10:46,712
There's a debate around it.

272
00:10:46,779 --> 00:10:49,015
No one gave another person a break.

273
00:10:49,081 --> 00:10:51,350
We starred in the content together
and both did well.

274
00:10:52,518 --> 00:10:53,352
However,

275
00:10:53,919 --> 00:10:56,055
if I had to point out the order…

276
00:10:56,122 --> 00:10:59,392
-There it is.
-I should have this ready.

277
00:10:59,458 --> 00:11:01,861
-If he had to point it out.
-If he had to.

278
00:11:01,927 --> 00:11:04,830
-I was the first…
-You were the first?

279
00:11:05,665 --> 00:11:06,565
…to lead her.

280
00:11:06,632 --> 00:11:08,267
-Gosh.
-But now,

281
00:11:08,334 --> 00:11:11,070
Rescene is pulling
both Yoo Young-woo and me

282
00:11:11,137 --> 00:11:13,072
into the spotlight.

283
00:11:13,139 --> 00:11:15,508
I'm so grateful
that she's doing that for us in return.

284
00:11:15,574 --> 00:11:18,144
-I think that's the situation.
-Sure.

285
00:11:18,210 --> 00:11:20,346
After all, you're saying

286
00:11:20,413 --> 00:11:23,482
you were the first
to notice Woni's talent.

287
00:11:23,549 --> 00:11:26,419
-Well, she's so talented.
-Oh, no.

288
00:11:26,485 --> 00:11:29,789
We've both been lucky.

289
00:11:29,855 --> 00:11:31,257
So she just got lucky?

290
00:11:31,323 --> 00:11:32,291
Sorry?

291
00:11:32,958 --> 00:11:35,494
-It was sheer luck?
-No, I've mentioned it already.

292
00:11:35,561 --> 00:11:37,063
She's so talented.

293
00:11:37,129 --> 00:11:38,064
It wasn't her effort?

294
00:11:38,130 --> 00:11:41,901
I'm sure she has put in
unimaginable amount of effort.

295
00:11:41,967 --> 00:11:43,969
-It's not just luck, right?
-Of course not.

296
00:11:45,371 --> 00:11:46,672
-Was that okay?
-Yes.

297
00:11:46,739 --> 00:11:48,140
WHY DON'T WE WRAP IT UP?

298
00:11:48,808 --> 00:11:52,878
Seon-min bought a meal for Woni
at a fine dining restaurant.

299
00:11:52,945 --> 00:11:54,480
-I see.
-It was for a video.

300
00:11:54,547 --> 00:11:55,815
How did that happen?

301
00:11:55,881 --> 00:11:58,150
We had a chat
while eating some <i>gukbap</i>, soup rice.

302
00:11:58,217 --> 00:12:01,454
If the view goes over 200,000,
I'll take you to a fine dining restaurant.

303
00:12:01,520 --> 00:12:04,590
-I made a pledge that I'd take her.
-That sounds great.

304
00:12:04,657 --> 00:12:06,692
It went over 200,000 views in 3 days,

305
00:12:06,759 --> 00:12:08,894
and ended up reaching over 400,000 views.

306
00:12:08,961 --> 00:12:12,364
So I took her to a fine dining restaurant
and ate together.

307
00:12:13,299 --> 00:12:15,334
AWESOME♥

308
00:12:15,401 --> 00:12:18,704
That video reached 6.24 million views now.

309
00:12:18,771 --> 00:12:21,006
The visit to the fine dining restaurant.

310
00:12:21,073 --> 00:12:24,710
I think Woni owes it to you too.

311
00:12:24,777 --> 00:12:27,346
She should buy me a meal now.

312
00:12:27,413 --> 00:12:29,315
-Right?
-What?

313
00:12:29,381 --> 00:12:31,050
Was that a mistake?

314
00:12:31,350 --> 00:12:33,119
REALIZES IT IMMEDIATELY

315
00:12:33,185 --> 00:12:35,688
-It might've been.
-Turn that on now.

316
00:12:36,589 --> 00:12:38,491
-It's the end of his era.
-You're very cautious.

317
00:12:38,557 --> 00:12:39,925
GOODBYE, LEE SEON-MIN

318
00:12:39,992 --> 00:12:42,862
I heard you were quite surprised
at the restaurant.

319
00:12:42,928 --> 00:12:44,563
Because the food was so good.

320
00:12:44,630 --> 00:12:46,198
-I see.
-Was it your first time?

321
00:12:46,265 --> 00:12:48,901
-It was my first experience.
-Your first time.

322
00:12:48,968 --> 00:12:51,804
I realized why people choose

323
00:12:51,871 --> 00:12:53,906
to go to these restaurants
for such an experience.

324
00:12:53,973 --> 00:12:56,275
But I haven't had any since then.

325
00:12:56,342 --> 00:12:59,845
So now that I'm on the show,
I want to have a taste

326
00:12:59,912 --> 00:13:03,649
-of the chefs' luxurious cuisine.
-They're the best.

327
00:13:03,716 --> 00:13:05,918
They can provide
an amazing fine dining experience.

328
00:13:05,985 --> 00:13:08,687
You'll get a taste of that today.

329
00:13:08,754 --> 00:13:11,123
I'm looking forward to seeing your fridge.

330
00:13:11,190 --> 00:13:13,559
You're known to have married man energy,

331
00:13:13,626 --> 00:13:14,994
but you do live alone, right?

332
00:13:15,060 --> 00:13:16,662
Yes, I do.

333
00:13:16,729 --> 00:13:18,063
How long has it been?

334
00:13:18,130 --> 00:13:20,766
Ever since I moved to Seoul,
so almost 19 years now.

335
00:13:20,833 --> 00:13:22,668
-It's his 19th year.
-Almost 20 years.

336
00:13:22,735 --> 00:13:24,870
Then you must be
good at cooking for yourself.

337
00:13:24,937 --> 00:13:28,107
I like to cook for myself at home,

338
00:13:28,174 --> 00:13:31,210
but I've been
too busy recently to do that,

339
00:13:31,277 --> 00:13:32,878
so I had to eat out.

340
00:13:32,945 --> 00:13:35,447
So I'm not so sure
if my ingredients are fresh enough.

341
00:13:35,514 --> 00:13:36,615
HMM…

342
00:13:36,682 --> 00:13:37,616
Is this a warning?

343
00:13:38,184 --> 00:13:39,084
Not exactly.

344
00:13:39,151 --> 00:13:41,120
-I'm just being honest.
-I see.

345
00:13:42,288 --> 00:13:43,122
All right.

346
00:13:43,189 --> 00:13:46,325
What kind of ingredients
does he have in his fridge?

347
00:13:46,392 --> 00:13:48,327
-We present Seon-min's fridge!
-Fridge!

348
00:13:48,661 --> 00:13:50,396
AMAZED

349
00:13:51,297 --> 00:13:52,364
THE FRIDGE LOOKS FULL

350
00:13:52,431 --> 00:13:53,399
It looks like my fridge.

351
00:13:53,465 --> 00:13:56,969
It's emptier than usual, actually.

352
00:13:57,036 --> 00:13:58,504
-Is it?
-I usually have more.

353
00:13:58,571 --> 00:13:59,572
-More than this?
-Much more.

354
00:13:59,638 --> 00:14:00,506
It looks neat.

355
00:14:00,573 --> 00:14:01,607
The first shelf.

356
00:14:01,674 --> 00:14:05,044
-Protein drinks.
-"Milk protein."

357
00:14:05,110 --> 00:14:06,779
When do you drink this?

358
00:14:06,846 --> 00:14:08,747
I don't really eat late-night snacks.

359
00:14:08,814 --> 00:14:10,816
Instead, I drink a bottle of that
and fall asleep.

360
00:14:10,883 --> 00:14:12,518
-After exercising outdoors?
-Yes.

361
00:14:12,585 --> 00:14:14,720
-Why don't you believe me?
-No.

362
00:14:14,787 --> 00:14:16,856
I've seen you eat
late-night snacks pretty often.

363
00:14:17,790 --> 00:14:19,592
I've cut them down significantly.

364
00:14:19,658 --> 00:14:22,094
-Compared to before?
-He's staying in shape.

365
00:14:22,428 --> 00:14:23,863
STARING…

366
00:14:23,929 --> 00:14:26,999
This expired on July 4th.

367
00:14:27,066 --> 00:14:28,734
-July 4th?
-July 4th.

368
00:14:28,801 --> 00:14:31,403
-That's still fresh.
-Is it?

369
00:14:31,470 --> 00:14:34,106
As long as it's in the fridge

370
00:14:34,173 --> 00:14:36,675
and remains unopened,

371
00:14:37,243 --> 00:14:40,079
I think it's okay to consume it
after three to four months.

372
00:14:40,145 --> 00:14:41,714
-Really?
-No, it's not.

373
00:14:41,780 --> 00:14:43,816
-That's pretty much brand new.
-Brand new?

374
00:14:43,883 --> 00:14:47,453
I can eat that without getting too sick.

375
00:14:47,519 --> 00:14:49,655
I'm curious to find out
what the rest are like.

376
00:14:49,722 --> 00:14:51,557
You must've bought all these together.

377
00:14:51,624 --> 00:14:55,127
-The second shelf has side dishes.
-Side dishes.

378
00:14:55,194 --> 00:14:57,630
I don't think you made these yourself.

379
00:14:57,696 --> 00:15:01,100
My parents sent that anchovy dish for me.

380
00:15:01,166 --> 00:15:02,902
-Anchovy dish.
-From Gumi.

381
00:15:02,968 --> 00:15:06,505
Do you often eat Korean food
for lunch and dinner?

382
00:15:06,572 --> 00:15:09,842
I'm mostly into Korean food,
so yes, that's what I eat.

383
00:15:09,909 --> 00:15:12,111
Yes, and some garlic.

384
00:15:12,177 --> 00:15:13,245
Whoa!

385
00:15:13,312 --> 00:15:14,413
-What?
-What is it?

386
00:15:14,713 --> 00:15:16,849
FOUND SOMETHING

387
00:15:17,216 --> 00:15:18,083
NERVOUS

388
00:15:18,150 --> 00:15:20,352
-We haven't seen this in a while.
-Don't open it.

389
00:15:20,419 --> 00:15:23,489
We haven't seen
a side dish like this for a while.

390
00:15:23,555 --> 00:15:25,724
-What is it?
-Perilla leaves.

391
00:15:25,791 --> 00:15:27,092
But look.

392
00:15:27,359 --> 00:15:30,529
FIRST SNOW ON PERILLA LEAVES

393
00:15:30,863 --> 00:15:31,964
SMH…

394
00:15:32,331 --> 00:15:33,632
SEEING HIS FIRST SNOW

395
00:15:33,699 --> 00:15:35,768
They rarely go bad
because of the salt level.

396
00:15:35,834 --> 00:15:37,703
Yes, that rarely happens
for perilla leaves.

397
00:15:37,770 --> 00:15:39,238
You should throw this out.

398
00:15:39,305 --> 00:15:42,308
Mold is all over that thing.

399
00:15:42,374 --> 00:15:44,643
Wait, it looks like
he's asking us not to throw it out.

400
00:15:44,710 --> 00:15:45,811
-What?
-You have to.

401
00:15:45,878 --> 00:15:47,146
Are you growing the mold?

402
00:15:47,212 --> 00:15:48,247
It must've grown on him.

403
00:15:48,314 --> 00:15:50,282
Can't I just remove the mold and eat it?

404
00:15:50,349 --> 00:15:51,717
-No!
-You can't.

405
00:15:51,784 --> 00:15:53,085
-No?
-No.

406
00:15:53,152 --> 00:15:54,520
-No.
-No.

407
00:15:54,920 --> 00:15:57,623
EDDIE'S QUITE SHOCKED TOO…

408
00:15:57,690 --> 00:15:58,958
Can't he just throw away the top leaf?

409
00:15:59,024 --> 00:15:59,992
-No!
-No!

410
00:16:00,059 --> 00:16:01,760
How many leaves do you see underneath?

411
00:16:01,827 --> 00:16:03,128
The thing is…

412
00:16:03,195 --> 00:16:05,731
You still have five to six leaves left.

413
00:16:05,798 --> 00:16:08,033
I can eat half a bowl of rice with those.

414
00:16:08,100 --> 00:16:09,902
NO… PLEASE THROW IT OUT…

415
00:16:09,969 --> 00:16:13,205
But I'll start making a habit
of throwing out

416
00:16:13,272 --> 00:16:16,008
-items that are too moldy for my health.
-You're really big now.

417
00:16:16,075 --> 00:16:17,943
-For your health.
-For health.

418
00:16:18,010 --> 00:16:20,746
It's not good for you.
It'll take longer for you to recover.

419
00:16:20,813 --> 00:16:23,782
These aren't just normal side dishes.
We should take a look at everything.

420
00:16:23,849 --> 00:16:25,651
-What is this?
-What?

421
00:16:25,718 --> 00:16:27,086
-Gosh!
-Gosh!

422
00:16:27,152 --> 00:16:29,288
-What is it?
-More mold?

423
00:16:29,655 --> 00:16:31,523
SNOW ON SWEET POTATO STEMS

424
00:16:31,590 --> 00:16:33,993
It shouldn't have surface yeast.

425
00:16:34,059 --> 00:16:37,029
-I mean, that's not kimchi.
-It's like a drop of mayonnaise.

426
00:16:37,096 --> 00:16:37,997
Mayonnaise?

427
00:16:38,063 --> 00:16:40,933
-I've never mixed it in.
-You haven't, right?

428
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:42,835
-I'll just… Yes.
-In here?

429
00:16:42,901 --> 00:16:44,937
-Please get rid of it.
-You can't eat that.

430
00:16:45,004 --> 00:16:46,638
I can tell by your face

431
00:16:46,705 --> 00:16:49,541
that you've often had it
after just removing the mold.

432
00:16:49,608 --> 00:16:51,143
I can't just throw it out

433
00:16:51,210 --> 00:16:55,047
when I think of the thought
put into that by the one who made it.

434
00:16:55,114 --> 00:16:56,715
Then you should've had it earlier.

435
00:16:56,782 --> 00:16:58,484
You're right.

436
00:16:58,550 --> 00:17:00,552
It'll shorten your life. Don't eat that.

437
00:17:00,619 --> 00:17:02,388
Don't even open that!

438
00:17:02,454 --> 00:17:03,956
-I can already tell.
-What is it?

439
00:17:04,923 --> 00:17:08,093
HEAVY SNOW

440
00:17:08,160 --> 00:17:09,895
What is this? <i>Jangjorim?</i>

441
00:17:09,962 --> 00:17:13,999
I think I remember.
Chuseok holidays have gone by, right?

442
00:17:14,066 --> 00:17:15,534
I got that in the Lunar New Year.

443
00:17:15,601 --> 00:17:17,069
-The Lunar New Year?
-The Lunar New Year?

444
00:17:17,136 --> 00:17:18,570
-The New Year?
-Chuseok has gone by.

445
00:17:18,637 --> 00:17:20,506
Don't ferment it like kimchi.

446
00:17:20,572 --> 00:17:22,074
Come on, Seon-min.

447
00:17:22,141 --> 00:17:23,642
-It's an emergency.
-At this moment…

448
00:17:23,709 --> 00:17:25,778
There's nothing we can use.

449
00:17:25,844 --> 00:17:28,414
Do you keep kimchi
in that red container back there?

450
00:17:28,480 --> 00:17:31,450
-Those are kimchi.
-He has kimchi here.

451
00:17:31,517 --> 00:17:33,185
Do you see this, everyone?

452
00:17:33,252 --> 00:17:36,155
That white plastic container.

453
00:17:36,221 --> 00:17:38,390
Do you see how it's bulging on the top?

454
00:17:38,457 --> 00:17:40,259
-It's puffing up.
-Goodness.

455
00:17:40,325 --> 00:17:42,694
We should…

456
00:17:42,761 --> 00:17:45,798
open that after
we call the emergency service.

457
00:17:45,864 --> 00:17:48,167
-That's what we should do.
-I'm feeling cautious.

458
00:17:48,233 --> 00:17:49,835
This is also kimchi.

459
00:17:49,902 --> 00:17:52,938
It's still sealed,
but it was made on March 10th, 2026.

460
00:17:53,005 --> 00:17:55,140
-That's not too long ago.
-No.

461
00:17:55,207 --> 00:17:56,909
-This guy is pretty angry.
-This is…

462
00:17:56,975 --> 00:17:58,544
You should soothe it a little.

463
00:17:58,610 --> 00:18:00,345
Should we open it?

464
00:18:00,412 --> 00:18:01,980
-Don't.
-Just leave it sealed.

465
00:18:02,047 --> 00:18:05,651
-Be careful.
-That's extra spicy kimchi,

466
00:18:05,717 --> 00:18:07,953
so it's probably been salted well.

467
00:18:08,020 --> 00:18:10,722
-We're going live.
-If the package is bulging,

468
00:18:10,789 --> 00:18:12,157
it means it's been fermented.

469
00:18:12,224 --> 00:18:14,493
-For the owner.
-Lactic acid needs oxygen.

470
00:18:14,560 --> 00:18:17,196
-Are you serious?
-The owner will open it himself.

471
00:18:17,262 --> 00:18:18,497
-Wait.
-All right, everyone.

472
00:18:18,564 --> 00:18:20,732
-Oh, my gosh.
-"Oh, my gosh."

473
00:18:20,799 --> 00:18:23,235
-Edward went, "Oh, my gosh."
-Give him space.

474
00:18:23,435 --> 00:18:25,637
KEEP A SAFE DISTANCE

475
00:18:25,704 --> 00:18:27,806
Can I throw this?

476
00:18:28,140 --> 00:18:29,641
EVEN THE OWNER'S SCARED

477
00:18:29,708 --> 00:18:32,611
-Geez.
-Let the air out first.

478
00:18:32,678 --> 00:18:35,347
-Lightly.
-That's it.

479
00:18:35,414 --> 00:18:37,483
OPENED SAFELY

480
00:18:38,150 --> 00:18:38,984
What's it like?

481
00:18:39,284 --> 00:18:40,619
SNIFF CHECK

482
00:18:40,686 --> 00:18:42,621
It's fresh!

483
00:18:42,688 --> 00:18:44,923
-Is it?
-It's as if it was made yesterday.

484
00:18:44,990 --> 00:18:46,959
-Is it okay?
-It really is. Smell it.

485
00:18:47,025 --> 00:18:49,561
I'm sure they have
expiration dates for a reason.

486
00:18:49,628 --> 00:18:51,296
-Doesn't it look fine?
-The color looks great.

487
00:18:51,363 --> 00:18:53,499
If something looks like this,
it's considered fresh,

488
00:18:53,565 --> 00:18:54,967
-"Fresh"?
-…like it was made yesterday.

489
00:18:55,200 --> 00:18:56,535
THEY CAN USE THE SPICY KIMCHI

490
00:18:56,602 --> 00:18:57,769
We finally found one ingredient.

491
00:18:58,137 --> 00:18:59,605
WHAT A RELIEF…

492
00:18:59,671 --> 00:19:02,074
Do you ever throw away your food?

493
00:19:02,141 --> 00:19:06,478
If it looks rotten on the outside, I do.

494
00:19:06,545 --> 00:19:09,548
But I recently didn't have time
to look through my fridge.

495
00:19:09,615 --> 00:19:11,416
-You were busy.
-He's been busy.

496
00:19:11,483 --> 00:19:13,385
-It's not usually like this, right?
-No.

497
00:19:13,452 --> 00:19:14,887
I usually use everything very fast.

498
00:19:14,953 --> 00:19:17,089
-He eats well.
-As quickly as fridges at restaurants.

499
00:19:17,156 --> 00:19:19,158
When you eat,

500
00:19:19,658 --> 00:19:22,794
do you tend to leave some food
or eat everything that's served?

501
00:19:22,861 --> 00:19:25,230
I even lick the sauce off the dish.

502
00:19:25,831 --> 00:19:28,000
-Yes.
-I finish everything.

503
00:19:28,066 --> 00:19:29,268
He's my kind.

504
00:19:29,334 --> 00:19:30,536
You're a human dishwasher.

505
00:19:30,602 --> 00:19:34,206
I am. I never leave leftovers.

506
00:19:34,273 --> 00:19:35,674
He's serious.

507
00:19:35,741 --> 00:19:37,709
He eats the desiccant in seaweed packets.

508
00:19:37,776 --> 00:19:39,545
-Come on.
-I'm serious.

509
00:19:39,611 --> 00:19:41,480
-I mean it.
-You eat the desiccant?

510
00:19:41,547 --> 00:19:43,916
-He eats moldy food, so…
-It's pretty good.

511
00:19:44,183 --> 00:19:45,684
DO NOT CONSUME DESICCANT

512
00:19:45,751 --> 00:19:48,554
All his plates look like this.

513
00:19:48,620 --> 00:19:51,023
If you gather all the leftovers

514
00:19:52,057 --> 00:19:55,594
from my time in the military,
it won't even fill a single tray.

515
00:19:55,661 --> 00:19:57,362
WON'T EVEN FILL A SINGLE TRAY

516
00:19:57,429 --> 00:19:59,164
-That's amazing.
-I'm serious.

517
00:19:59,231 --> 00:20:01,700
Do you eat
a little more than a typical serving?

518
00:20:01,767 --> 00:20:03,335
When I ate the most,

519
00:20:03,902 --> 00:20:05,737
I'd eat three bags of <i>ramyeon</i>

520
00:20:05,804 --> 00:20:07,773
and two bowls of rice with the soup.

521
00:20:07,839 --> 00:20:09,741
-What?
-He eats a lot.

522
00:20:09,808 --> 00:20:11,443
And a quarter head of kimchi.

523
00:20:12,010 --> 00:20:14,880
I have a big appetite.
And with that, two liters of soda.

524
00:20:14,947 --> 00:20:17,349
-That's so nice.
-Two liters?

525
00:20:17,416 --> 00:20:19,985
What was your nickname in school?

526
00:20:20,052 --> 00:20:20,953
I was a member

527
00:20:21,019 --> 00:20:23,822
-of the four kings of school lunch.
-"Four kings of school lunch"?

528
00:20:23,889 --> 00:20:25,324
Who were the other three?

529
00:20:25,390 --> 00:20:27,359
I've always been one of the top four kids

530
00:20:27,426 --> 00:20:30,395
-who ate well throughout my school days.
-You had a big appetite.

531
00:20:30,462 --> 00:20:32,931
Nam-no, did you also have a nickname
for eating well?

532
00:20:32,998 --> 00:20:37,803
Once I get hooked on a certain restaurant,
I only go there for a while.

533
00:20:37,869 --> 00:20:39,571
So my nickname was

534
00:20:39,638 --> 00:20:41,373
Back Again.

535
00:20:41,440 --> 00:20:43,609
-Back Again?
-Like, "He's back again"?

536
00:20:43,675 --> 00:20:45,077
-"Nam-no's back again"?
-Back Again.

537
00:20:45,143 --> 00:20:48,780
The staff would go, "He's back again.
Stock up on the side dishes."

538
00:20:48,847 --> 00:20:51,016
-Stock up on the side dishes.
-"He's back again."

539
00:20:51,083 --> 00:20:52,484
-Back Again.
-We would've been

540
00:20:52,551 --> 00:20:54,152
the best pair
if we went to school together.

541
00:20:55,053 --> 00:20:56,622
He would've been one of the four kings.

542
00:20:56,688 --> 00:20:58,290
-Right.
-Yes, one of the four kings.

543
00:20:58,357 --> 00:21:00,425
King of School Lunch, and Back Again.

544
00:21:00,492 --> 00:21:02,461
-Gosh.
-What's down here?

545
00:21:02,527 --> 00:21:04,029
I see some <i>doenjang</i> here.

546
00:21:04,096 --> 00:21:05,864
Did you make <i>doenjang-jjigae?</i>

547
00:21:05,931 --> 00:21:07,432
-He used most of it.
-Homemade <i>doenjang.</i>

548
00:21:07,499 --> 00:21:08,500
He used a lot.

549
00:21:09,067 --> 00:21:10,168
Gosh.

550
00:21:10,235 --> 00:21:13,005
"Best before: May 16th, 2023."

551
00:21:13,071 --> 00:21:16,141
It's been over three years.

552
00:21:16,208 --> 00:21:18,110
It's been fermenting nicely.

553
00:21:18,176 --> 00:21:19,411
Is it?

554
00:21:19,478 --> 00:21:21,046
This doesn't look store-bought.

555
00:21:21,113 --> 00:21:23,649
Did someone make this?

556
00:21:23,715 --> 00:21:27,519
My mother made it herself,

557
00:21:27,586 --> 00:21:28,987
and I've had it for eight years.

558
00:21:29,054 --> 00:21:30,989
Whoa. It's pungent.

559
00:21:31,056 --> 00:21:32,090
It looks good.

560
00:21:32,157 --> 00:21:33,792
Man, that's strong.

561
00:21:33,859 --> 00:21:35,961
-Come here if you have sinusitis.
-It'll open your nostrils.

562
00:21:36,028 --> 00:21:37,629
I can smell it from here.

563
00:21:37,696 --> 00:21:42,034
Eddie might run away
after one whiff of this.

564
00:21:42,100 --> 00:21:43,068
Shall we smell it?

565
00:21:43,135 --> 00:21:44,269
Yeah.

566
00:21:44,336 --> 00:21:46,571
-I love strong <i>doenjang.</i>
-You do?

567
00:21:47,005 --> 00:21:48,507
EDDIE'S CHALLENGE

568
00:21:48,573 --> 00:21:50,742
An eight-year-old <i>doenjang</i> for Eddie.

569
00:21:50,809 --> 00:21:52,277
It smells so good.

570
00:21:53,011 --> 00:21:54,212
Whoa!

571
00:21:54,279 --> 00:21:55,414
That's pungent.

572
00:21:55,480 --> 00:21:56,715
HE'S EVEN TASTING IT!

573
00:21:57,616 --> 00:21:58,450
It's been eight years.

574
00:21:58,517 --> 00:22:00,218
-Can you eat it?
-He ate it like it's caramel.

575
00:22:00,285 --> 00:22:02,587
-It's a bit salty.
-It's a bit salty?

576
00:22:02,654 --> 00:22:03,522
Homemade <i>doenjang</i> usually is.

577
00:22:03,588 --> 00:22:04,790
-And the taste?
-It's great.

578
00:22:04,856 --> 00:22:06,058
It's the real deal.

579
00:22:06,124 --> 00:22:08,760
Excuse me. Who's getting Eddie's shot?

580
00:22:09,094 --> 00:22:10,862
OVER HERE

581
00:22:10,929 --> 00:22:13,532
Keep filming him
and see if he shows any changes.

582
00:22:13,598 --> 00:22:16,735
Please keep an eye on him.
We might miss it,

583
00:22:16,802 --> 00:22:19,738
so watch him and see
if he shows any symptoms.

584
00:22:19,805 --> 00:22:20,672
See if he gets rashes.

585
00:22:20,739 --> 00:22:22,374
-Just like the sweet potato stems.
-Yes.

586
00:22:22,441 --> 00:22:24,810
-Man.
-It's been here for eight years.

587
00:22:24,876 --> 00:22:28,613
I've been eating that
since before I moved to my current place.

588
00:22:28,680 --> 00:22:31,883
Then it would've had to sit outside
on the moving day.

589
00:22:31,950 --> 00:22:34,553
While I moved,

590
00:22:34,619 --> 00:22:37,155
it got to breathe a little.

591
00:22:37,222 --> 00:22:39,458
It probably felt refreshed.

592
00:22:39,524 --> 00:22:41,059
That would double the flavor.

593
00:22:41,126 --> 00:22:44,229
You must be a pretty good cook yourself
if you care about your <i>doenjang.</i>

594
00:22:44,296 --> 00:22:47,199
I make soup, <i>doenjang-guk</i>,
when my friends come over.

595
00:22:47,265 --> 00:22:48,600
They go, "How is this so good?"

596
00:22:48,834 --> 00:22:51,002
EVERYONE LOVES THE 8-YEAR-OLD DOENJANG

597
00:22:51,069 --> 00:22:53,705
You have anchovies for broth
and some others.

598
00:22:53,772 --> 00:22:55,307
So you must make broth with these.

599
00:22:55,374 --> 00:22:57,376
I'm the type who has to
make the broth myself

600
00:22:57,442 --> 00:22:58,944
for soup dishes like <i>jjigae</i> or <i>jeongol.</i>

601
00:23:00,178 --> 00:23:01,446
For that <i>ramyeon </i>with kelp,

602
00:23:01,513 --> 00:23:03,682
I think it needs some more flavor,

603
00:23:03,749 --> 00:23:05,984
so I throw in a few more pieces.

604
00:23:06,051 --> 00:23:07,486
And some eggs.

605
00:23:07,552 --> 00:23:09,821
-White eggs.
-Are they peeled?

606
00:23:09,888 --> 00:23:12,090
I recently went to a supermarket
and saw white eggs

607
00:23:12,157 --> 00:23:13,425
were half the price of brown ones.

608
00:23:13,492 --> 00:23:15,260
A FRUGAL PURCHASE

609
00:23:15,327 --> 00:23:16,928
Do you go grocery shopping?

610
00:23:16,995 --> 00:23:18,764
I like to buy things in person.

611
00:23:19,731 --> 00:23:22,701
I have to see the items for myself
and touch them too.

612
00:23:22,768 --> 00:23:25,137
It makes me trust the produce more,
and it's fun too.

613
00:23:25,203 --> 00:23:26,638
And haggle over the price?

614
00:23:26,705 --> 00:23:28,940
I'd get in trouble
if I did that at a major supermarket.

615
00:23:29,574 --> 00:23:31,710
The security will kick me out.

616
00:23:31,777 --> 00:23:35,213
-How about at a local market?
-I do that from time to time.

617
00:23:35,280 --> 00:23:39,184
I see some veggies.
You did a great job at storing them.

618
00:23:39,251 --> 00:23:42,721
-I tend to portion them out.
-You do.

619
00:23:42,788 --> 00:23:46,091
I'm a healthy big guy
who knows the importance of veggies.

620
00:23:46,158 --> 00:23:47,959
-I pay attention.
-To stay healthy.

621
00:23:48,026 --> 00:23:49,394
And fruit.

622
00:23:49,461 --> 00:23:51,897
He pre-portioned the fruit too.

623
00:23:52,964 --> 00:23:53,799
Ugh!

624
00:23:53,865 --> 00:23:55,367
-What?
-What is it?

625
00:23:55,434 --> 00:23:56,268
Mold?

626
00:23:56,334 --> 00:23:57,869
GLANCING

627
00:23:58,670 --> 00:23:59,905
I'm glad we spotted this.

628
00:23:59,971 --> 00:24:01,173
What is it?

629
00:24:01,239 --> 00:24:03,775
-What is that?
-That looks bad.

630
00:24:03,842 --> 00:24:05,777
-Cherries?
-Cherries?

631
00:24:05,844 --> 00:24:08,246
-These cherries have wilted.
-They've turned into juice.

632
00:24:08,313 --> 00:24:09,714
He must be making wine.

633
00:24:09,781 --> 00:24:12,017
-These cherries…
-It really smells like alcohol.

634
00:24:12,350 --> 00:24:13,785
AGED NICELY

635
00:24:13,852 --> 00:24:16,054
-It does.
-This juice might taste like wine.

636
00:24:16,121 --> 00:24:17,355
-I'm curious.
-Cherry liquor.

637
00:24:17,422 --> 00:24:19,124
Isn't it okay?

638
00:24:19,191 --> 00:24:23,428
-It's not.
-It's moldy. How is this okay?

639
00:24:23,495 --> 00:24:26,698
-Let's throw this out.
-We have to.

640
00:24:26,765 --> 00:24:27,699
The freezer.

641
00:24:27,766 --> 00:24:29,134
It should be fine.

642
00:24:29,201 --> 00:24:30,936
I see some meat.

643
00:24:31,002 --> 00:24:33,638
Have they been sitting in here
for a while? They're frosty.

644
00:24:33,705 --> 00:24:36,107
You can trust me and use them.

645
00:24:36,942 --> 00:24:39,110
ACTING SMUG FOR THE FIRST TIME

646
00:24:39,177 --> 00:24:40,479
He can finally say that proudly.

647
00:24:40,545 --> 00:24:41,880
They're all recent purchases.

648
00:24:41,947 --> 00:24:44,349
I see lots of seafood like mackerel…

649
00:24:44,416 --> 00:24:46,117
-He has abalones.
-…and abalones.

650
00:24:46,184 --> 00:24:48,753
It's only been two weeks
since I put those shrimp and abalones

651
00:24:48,820 --> 00:24:50,522
in the freezer.

652
00:24:50,822 --> 00:24:52,290
VERY FRESH BY SEON-MIN'S STANDARD

653
00:24:52,357 --> 00:24:54,926
What do you prefer? Meat or seafood?

654
00:24:54,993 --> 00:24:58,363
I prefer seafood a little more,
in about a six to four ratio.

655
00:24:58,430 --> 00:25:00,365
-Six to four.
-I love seafood so much,

656
00:25:00,432 --> 00:25:02,033
so I always keep them around.

657
00:25:02,100 --> 00:25:03,468
Next, shelf-stable ingredients.

658
00:25:03,535 --> 00:25:05,036
Shelf-stable ingredients.

659
00:25:05,103 --> 00:25:06,872
Why do you have a huge pack
of dried seaweed?

660
00:25:06,938 --> 00:25:08,139
To make lots of birthday soup?

661
00:25:08,206 --> 00:25:10,642
I throw myself
a birthday party once a month.

662
00:25:10,976 --> 00:25:13,178
JUST TO EAT MIYEOK-GUK, SEAWEED SOUP

663
00:25:13,245 --> 00:25:14,913
When I eat <i>miyeok-guk,</i>

664
00:25:14,980 --> 00:25:16,414
I need to eat a huge portion.

665
00:25:16,481 --> 00:25:18,250
-I have to eat it like a whale.
-"Like a whale"?

666
00:25:18,316 --> 00:25:20,385
SOUNDS DELICIOUS♥

667
00:25:21,286 --> 00:25:22,954
I have to scoop it up like this.

668
00:25:23,021 --> 00:25:25,957
-And a big bag of <i>siraegi,</i> radish stems.
-He has <i>siraegi?</i>

669
00:25:26,024 --> 00:25:27,926
Expiration dates are something

670
00:25:27,993 --> 00:25:29,628
that humans just decide on.

671
00:25:29,995 --> 00:25:31,096
WHY WOULD YOU DECIDE THAT?

672
00:25:31,162 --> 00:25:32,430
Why would humans decide it?

673
00:25:32,497 --> 00:25:35,300
I think those can last
for over five years.

674
00:25:35,367 --> 00:25:38,570
If you think something will last long,

675
00:25:38,637 --> 00:25:40,005
-you buy it in bulk.
-That's right.

676
00:25:40,071 --> 00:25:42,307
-That's much cheaper.
-Yes, like dried food.

677
00:25:42,374 --> 00:25:45,343
After living alone for years, I've become
highly sensitive to cost efficiency.

678
00:25:45,410 --> 00:25:47,412
-You have lots of rice.
-Why do you have so much?

679
00:25:47,479 --> 00:25:49,748
I always make my rice
when I have enough time.

680
00:25:49,814 --> 00:25:52,317
-I see.
-So I always keep some rice around.

681
00:25:52,384 --> 00:25:54,486
-And beans.
-Black lentils.

682
00:25:54,553 --> 00:25:56,221
I'm suffering from hair loss.

683
00:25:56,488 --> 00:25:57,756
SUDDENLY SERIOUS

684
00:25:57,822 --> 00:26:02,260
I heard lentils are good for you,
so I've been mixing them in.

685
00:26:02,327 --> 00:26:06,598
That was all for Seon-min's fridge,
a frugal man and an expert in solo living.

686
00:26:07,832 --> 00:26:09,301
The man who lives alone.

687
00:26:09,367 --> 00:26:12,504
It's time to see the orders he has put in.

688
00:26:12,571 --> 00:26:15,440
Show us his orders!

689
00:26:16,207 --> 00:26:17,142
The first one.

690
00:26:17,208 --> 00:26:19,544
Do I Have the Face of Success?

691
00:26:19,611 --> 00:26:22,314
I've only recently experienced
the world of fine dining,

692
00:26:22,380 --> 00:26:24,950
and I've only had it once,
but it was such a shock.

693
00:26:25,016 --> 00:26:26,885
THE TASTE OF SUCCESS CAME AS A SHOCK

694
00:26:26,952 --> 00:26:29,454
I wondered what it would be like
to eat luxurious dishes

695
00:26:29,521 --> 00:26:32,223
that are made
by the hands of Korea's best chefs.

696
00:26:32,290 --> 00:26:34,726
So I want to eat fine dining dishes

697
00:26:34,793 --> 00:26:37,028
that'll make me mesmerized.

698
00:26:37,862 --> 00:26:39,965
What's the second order?

699
00:26:40,832 --> 00:26:42,801
-Dishwasher Energy.
-Energy.

700
00:26:43,201 --> 00:26:44,035
DISHWASHER ENERGY?

701
00:26:44,102 --> 00:26:45,270
A dishwasher?

702
00:26:45,337 --> 00:26:47,038
-Yes.
-What's this about?

703
00:26:47,105 --> 00:26:50,108
It's a hassle to do the dishes
when you live alone.

704
00:26:50,175 --> 00:26:53,144
-Yes.
-So help me have fewer dirty dishes

705
00:26:54,112 --> 00:26:58,416
by making me something so good
that I'll eat up every drop of sauce.

706
00:26:59,150 --> 00:27:01,853
We should probably ask Nam-no to pick one.

707
00:27:01,920 --> 00:27:04,222
The first choice goes to him.

708
00:27:04,289 --> 00:27:05,457
What'll it be?

709
00:27:06,091 --> 00:27:08,159
It's Dishwasher Energy for me.

710
00:27:08,493 --> 00:27:09,728
GLAD♥

711
00:27:09,794 --> 00:27:13,632
We could've been
the kings of school lunch together.

712
00:27:13,698 --> 00:27:16,401
-The King and Back Again.
-Back Again and the King.

713
00:27:16,468 --> 00:27:20,038
Nam-no takes a very promising spot.

714
00:27:20,105 --> 00:27:22,040
PLEASED

715
00:27:22,374 --> 00:27:23,942
WHO'LL GO NEXT?

716
00:27:24,009 --> 00:27:25,110
I'll go next.

717
00:27:25,176 --> 00:27:27,879
-Eun-yeong? Where?
-I may not have the dishwasher energy,

718
00:27:27,946 --> 00:27:30,415
but I make really great sauce.

719
00:27:30,482 --> 00:27:32,083
So I'll help you scrape every drop of it

720
00:27:32,150 --> 00:27:35,553
while I show Nam-no
what it's like to make you do the dishes.

721
00:27:35,620 --> 00:27:37,756
-You're joining to take me down?
-Nam-no?

722
00:27:37,822 --> 00:27:39,624
-Yes.
-Taking him down.

723
00:27:39,691 --> 00:27:41,359
She's not scared of Nam-no.

724
00:27:41,426 --> 00:27:43,261
What about the taste of success?

725
00:27:43,328 --> 00:27:45,230
-He's the big shot now.
-He is a success.

726
00:27:45,296 --> 00:27:47,165
-Fine dining.
-A fine dining experience.

727
00:27:47,232 --> 00:27:49,000
-Hey.
-Chef Eddie?

728
00:27:49,768 --> 00:27:51,670
I'll join.

729
00:27:51,736 --> 00:27:53,938
A dish that can turn you into a success.

730
00:27:54,005 --> 00:27:55,573
But with those ingredients?

731
00:27:55,640 --> 00:27:57,342
RAISES HIS HAND IMMEDIATELY!

732
00:27:57,409 --> 00:27:58,677
-I'll go.
-Canine?

733
00:27:58,743 --> 00:28:01,446
-This is a big deal.
-I've never faced him before,

734
00:28:01,513 --> 00:28:03,014
so I want to challenge him.

735
00:28:03,081 --> 00:28:05,417
-Is this your first cook-off?
-Yes.

736
00:28:05,483 --> 00:28:07,852
-You've never faced Chef Edward?
-This will be fun.

737
00:28:08,219 --> 00:28:10,422
THE MASTER CHEFS
WILL SHOW HIM THE TASTE OF SUCCESS

738
00:28:10,488 --> 00:28:12,390
The matchups are set.

739
00:28:12,457 --> 00:28:14,225
"Do I Have the Face of Success?"

740
00:28:14,292 --> 00:28:17,562
Let us begin the round
between Edward and Sam!

741
00:28:21,166 --> 00:28:25,470
<i>This is the first round of cook-off</i>
<i>played with Seon-min's ingredients.</i>

742
00:28:25,537 --> 00:28:27,105
Filled with brave lion energy,

743
00:28:27,172 --> 00:28:29,674
-Chef Edward…
-The lion energy.

744
00:28:29,741 --> 00:28:34,112
…versus suspicious canine energy,
Chef Sam Kim.

745
00:28:34,179 --> 00:28:35,346
Chef Eddie.

746
00:28:35,413 --> 00:28:38,750
Have you heard
about the so-called Canine incidents?

747
00:28:38,817 --> 00:28:42,120
<i>You've been away for a while,</i>
<i>and we've had a few unfortunate incidents.</i>

748
00:28:42,187 --> 00:28:44,089
-I've watched the episodes.
-You have?

749
00:28:44,389 --> 00:28:46,091
THE CANINE INCIDENT REACHES AMERICA

750
00:28:46,157 --> 00:28:47,592
Even people overseas know.

751
00:28:47,659 --> 00:28:51,229
I always watch
<i>Chef & My Fridge</i> in America.

752
00:28:51,296 --> 00:28:52,697
You do?

753
00:28:52,764 --> 00:28:55,166
I watch it, thinking,
"I want to be there."

754
00:28:55,233 --> 00:28:56,801
ALWAYS MISSING THE SHOW

755
00:28:56,868 --> 00:29:00,004
-Then you're already caught up.
-You already know.

756
00:29:00,071 --> 00:29:02,440
Check the electric tools
in case they don't work.

757
00:29:02,507 --> 00:29:05,310
-The oven and the fridge too.
-The blender.

758
00:29:05,376 --> 00:29:07,846
I'll be fine
as long as the gas range is working.

759
00:29:07,912 --> 00:29:09,447
-I can cook.
-The gas may be cut off.

760
00:29:09,514 --> 00:29:10,982
He might bite off the valve.

761
00:29:11,750 --> 00:29:13,718
-Bite. With his canine.
-Actually,

762
00:29:13,785 --> 00:29:16,955
-…I brought a letter here.
-A letter?

763
00:29:17,021 --> 00:29:20,391
<i>-It's a letter for Chef Sam.</i>
<i>-Okay.</i>

764
00:29:20,458 --> 00:29:23,328
He handed this to our production crew

765
00:29:23,394 --> 00:29:26,297
-and made sure that I read it.
-What is it?

766
00:29:26,364 --> 00:29:27,799
"Hello, Chef Sam.

767
00:29:27,866 --> 00:29:32,370
My name is Won Ji-u,
and I love to watch <i>Chef & My Fridge.</i>

768
00:29:32,437 --> 00:29:35,774
I believe the bingo mangsu incident

769
00:29:35,840 --> 00:29:38,176
and the microwave incident

770
00:29:38,243 --> 00:29:42,313
were not something that you did."

771
00:29:42,380 --> 00:29:44,082
Is this a petition?

772
00:29:44,516 --> 00:29:46,017
CAUGHT

773
00:29:46,084 --> 00:29:48,086
-It's as good as that.
-What is this?

774
00:29:48,153 --> 00:29:50,522
-He used a child.
-It's a petition.

775
00:29:50,588 --> 00:29:51,990
He could've written it himself.

776
00:29:52,056 --> 00:29:53,958
He could've written that
with his left hand.

777
00:29:54,025 --> 00:29:55,927
With his left hand?

778
00:29:55,994 --> 00:29:58,263
He could've imitated
a grade school kid's handwriting.

779
00:29:58,329 --> 00:29:59,597
-Well…
-Did you really get this?

780
00:29:59,664 --> 00:30:01,633
Many pre-teen children approach me

781
00:30:02,300 --> 00:30:04,235
and tell me that they believe me.

782
00:30:04,302 --> 00:30:05,737
That they believe in you?

783
00:30:05,804 --> 00:30:08,473
There are handwriting analysts
at the National Forensic Service,

784
00:30:08,540 --> 00:30:11,042
-so I'll send it there.
-Okay.

785
00:30:11,109 --> 00:30:12,844
<i>Please check that for us.</i>

786
00:30:12,911 --> 00:30:15,747
The situation
doesn't look great for either of them.

787
00:30:15,814 --> 00:30:18,149
Sam has lost three times in a row.

788
00:30:20,084 --> 00:30:20,919
It's an emergency.

789
00:30:20,985 --> 00:30:23,354
-And Chef Edward has no stars.
-No?

790
00:30:23,421 --> 00:30:25,356
Didn't he visit a few times this year?

791
00:30:25,423 --> 00:30:29,494
He did, but no stars. We're now seeing
two desperate chefs against each other.

792
00:30:29,561 --> 00:30:31,396
Can I really trust them with my food?

793
00:30:33,164 --> 00:30:34,399
Please do your best.

794
00:30:34,465 --> 00:30:35,400
They're good chefs.

795
00:30:35,466 --> 00:30:38,870
-I know.
-He won <i>Culinary Class Wars.</i>

796
00:30:38,937 --> 00:30:40,371
He won <i>Culinary Class Wars.</i>

797
00:30:40,438 --> 00:30:42,140
-I know that.
-Come on.

798
00:30:42,207 --> 00:30:43,474
DIDN'T NOTICE

799
00:30:43,541 --> 00:30:44,742
-Right.
-He finished second.

800
00:30:44,809 --> 00:30:46,411
Wait, yes. Second place of <i>CCW.</i>

801
00:30:46,477 --> 00:30:48,613
I'm so sorry!

802
00:30:48,680 --> 00:30:50,481
-He was first place.
-You won, right?

803
00:30:50,548 --> 00:30:54,385
-It's okay. First place is pretty good.
-You did well.

804
00:30:54,452 --> 00:30:56,187
-You did really well.
-I'm sorry.

805
00:30:56,254 --> 00:30:59,190
-He finished second…
-I always get confused.

806
00:30:59,257 --> 00:31:01,159
-You won.
-I wanted to finish second.

807
00:31:01,226 --> 00:31:02,961
I have a feeling

808
00:31:03,027 --> 00:31:05,730
this round will be
a bit more advantageous for Chef Edward.

809
00:31:05,797 --> 00:31:09,234
Why did you ask,
"Can I really trust him with my food?"

810
00:31:09,300 --> 00:31:11,102
I share my apology once again.

811
00:31:12,103 --> 00:31:15,540
-I now feel like a world leader.
-Exactly.

812
00:31:15,607 --> 00:31:19,077
I'll do my best
as if you were the president.

813
00:31:19,143 --> 00:31:21,880
-Thank you.
-As if you were the head of state.

814
00:31:21,946 --> 00:31:24,716
I'll cook as if he were my mother,
the one person I respect the most.

815
00:31:24,782 --> 00:31:26,251
TREATED LIKE HIS MOTHER

816
00:31:26,584 --> 00:31:28,219
BRINGS UP HIS MOTHER

817
00:31:28,286 --> 00:31:29,721
-You've changed.
-You're something.

818
00:31:29,787 --> 00:31:31,389
-She's important to me.
-Like his mother.

819
00:31:31,456 --> 00:31:33,691
He brought up a pre-teen fan,
and on top of that, his mother.

820
00:31:34,359 --> 00:31:36,261
DROPPING ALL HIS BEST MOVES

821
00:31:37,095 --> 00:31:39,130
CHEF EDWARD'S INGREDIENTS:
SHRIMP, ABALONE, SHREDDED DRIED POLLACK

822
00:31:39,197 --> 00:31:41,266
KELP, ANCHOVY, DRIED SHINGLED HEDGEHOG
TOMATO, ZUCCHINI, AND MORE

823
00:31:41,332 --> 00:31:44,702
First up, Chef Edward.
What are you making?

824
00:31:44,769 --> 00:31:47,805
This is a dish

825
00:31:47,872 --> 00:31:50,241
you can only eat once in your life.

826
00:31:50,308 --> 00:31:54,045
Edward LuxuLee Shrimp Pasta.

827
00:31:54,112 --> 00:31:57,448
-Edward LuxuLee Shrimp Pasta.
-Edward LuxuLee Shrimp Pasta.

828
00:31:57,515 --> 00:31:59,884
Edward Lee makes Edward LuxuLee.

829
00:31:59,951 --> 00:32:01,753
-Edward LuxuLee.
-Edward LuxuLee.

830
00:32:01,819 --> 00:32:03,521
EDWARD LUXULEE

831
00:32:03,588 --> 00:32:04,422
GASP

832
00:32:04,489 --> 00:32:06,557
So you're making shrimp pasta?

833
00:32:06,624 --> 00:32:08,359
Long ago,

834
00:32:08,426 --> 00:32:11,029
I traveled to France and Italy

835
00:32:11,095 --> 00:32:14,399
to learn how to make pasta
and French cuisine,

836
00:32:14,465 --> 00:32:16,868
two very important things.

837
00:32:16,935 --> 00:32:19,570
I'll use shredded dried pollack,
shingled hedgehog,

838
00:32:19,637 --> 00:32:22,707
abalone, and shrimp

839
00:32:22,774 --> 00:32:27,111
to make you shrimp scampi pasta.

840
00:32:27,178 --> 00:32:29,614
-Did you learn to make pasta in Europe?
-Yes.

841
00:32:29,681 --> 00:32:31,549
In southern France,

842
00:32:31,616 --> 00:32:33,952
you'll find many Italian restaurants
and Italian people.

843
00:32:34,185 --> 00:32:35,520
NODDING

844
00:32:35,586 --> 00:32:38,022
So I went there and learned
from an Italian grandmother.

845
00:32:38,089 --> 00:32:40,224
An Italian grandmother.

846
00:32:40,291 --> 00:32:41,459
A grandmother's recipe.

847
00:32:41,526 --> 00:32:42,894
It must be authentic.

848
00:32:42,961 --> 00:32:45,463
We're now talking about a grandmother too.

849
00:32:45,530 --> 00:32:47,098
That's the best move he could make.

850
00:32:47,398 --> 00:32:49,801
FIGHTING A LETHAL MOVE
WITH ANOTHER LETHAL MOVE

851
00:32:50,735 --> 00:32:52,737
CHEF SAM'S INGREDIENTS:
FLOUNDER, SHRIMP, WEBFOOT OCTOPUS

852
00:32:52,804 --> 00:32:54,806
TENDERLOIN, POTATO, TOMATO, CREAM CHEESE,
GAMTAE, ANCHOVY STIR-FRY, PERILLA OIL

853
00:32:54,872 --> 00:32:57,875
Chef Sam, what are you making
up against his dish?

854
00:32:57,942 --> 00:32:59,344
My dish is called…

855
00:32:59,844 --> 00:33:01,212
-What's that?
-He'll do something.

856
00:33:01,279 --> 00:33:03,881
…The Great Samtsby.

857
00:33:03,948 --> 00:33:05,883
-The Great Samtsby.
-The Great Samtsby.

858
00:33:05,950 --> 00:33:08,519
-SamCaprio.
-Not <i>The Great Gatsby.</i>

859
00:33:08,586 --> 00:33:10,321
I'll prepare two kinds of dishes.

860
00:33:10,388 --> 00:33:13,091
There's an Italian dish called carpaccio.

861
00:33:13,157 --> 00:33:16,661
I'll make a tomato version
and a meat version,

862
00:33:16,728 --> 00:33:19,063
so beef carpaccio and tomato carpaccio.

863
00:33:19,130 --> 00:33:21,666
As for the other one,

864
00:33:21,733 --> 00:33:23,568
I'll stuff flounder fillets
with ground shrimp

865
00:33:23,634 --> 00:33:25,603
and webfoot octopus, like a sandwich.

866
00:33:25,670 --> 00:33:27,338
-That sounds good.
-Flounder?

867
00:33:28,106 --> 00:33:29,540
How does he come up with that?

868
00:33:29,607 --> 00:33:31,642
-Right?
-All right, then.

869
00:33:31,709 --> 00:33:33,578
Let us begin their cook-off.

870
00:33:33,911 --> 00:33:35,813
AMAZED

871
00:33:35,880 --> 00:33:38,883
This round is called
"Do I Have the Face of Success?"

872
00:33:38,950 --> 00:33:42,053
He'll get a new taste of success today.

873
00:33:42,120 --> 00:33:45,356
"Do I look like
I'll win the first star today?"

874
00:33:45,423 --> 00:33:46,858
Chef Edward Lee.

875
00:33:46,924 --> 00:33:49,660
"Do I look like
I'll end my losing streak today?"

876
00:33:49,727 --> 00:33:51,796
Chef Sam Kim.

877
00:33:51,863 --> 00:33:54,298
Which one of the chefs
will successfully hunt the star?

878
00:33:54,365 --> 00:33:57,935
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

879
00:33:58,803 --> 00:34:00,104
Good luck!

880
00:34:00,738 --> 00:34:02,607
WHICH CHEF WILL SHOW HIM
THE TASTE OF SUCCESS?

881
00:34:02,940 --> 00:34:03,775
STARTING WITH FLOUNDER

882
00:34:03,841 --> 00:34:06,944
Sam starts by prepping the founder.

883
00:34:07,011 --> 00:34:09,113
-He said he'll make a sandwich.
-Yes.

884
00:34:09,180 --> 00:34:11,049
So will the fillets work as buns?

885
00:34:11,115 --> 00:34:13,518
Yes, I'll stuff it with the ingredients.

886
00:34:14,318 --> 00:34:15,520
How's the flounder looking?

887
00:34:15,586 --> 00:34:17,855
-Not so great.
-Not so great?

888
00:34:17,922 --> 00:34:21,025
-Must've been sitting there for a while.
-It must be old.

889
00:34:21,092 --> 00:34:23,094
-The color's a bit off.
-Is that half-dried flounder?

890
00:34:23,161 --> 00:34:24,762
No, it's not half-dried.

891
00:34:24,829 --> 00:34:27,732
-Has it been like three years?
-It's been frozen for a long time.

892
00:34:27,799 --> 00:34:29,367
Do you remember how old that is?

893
00:34:29,434 --> 00:34:30,868
That flounder…

894
00:34:30,935 --> 00:34:33,604
-Five to six months.
-It sat there for five to six months?

895
00:34:33,671 --> 00:34:36,340
Yes, but it's okay. That's fresh for me.

896
00:34:37,408 --> 00:34:40,044
You won't always get fresh ingredients.

897
00:34:40,111 --> 00:34:42,980
Edward starts his shrimp pasta.

898
00:34:43,614 --> 00:34:46,083
1. BOIL SHINGLED HEDGEHOG, GARLIC,
TOMATO, DRIED POLLACK, GREEN ONION

899
00:34:46,150 --> 00:34:47,218
Tomato.

900
00:34:48,686 --> 00:34:49,887
What did he just throw in?

901
00:34:49,954 --> 00:34:51,322
-What is it?
-Green onion?

902
00:34:51,389 --> 00:34:53,124
-And tomato?
-Green onion.

903
00:34:53,191 --> 00:34:54,859
-That's all?
-Is he boiling those?

904
00:34:54,926 --> 00:34:56,294
-What's that?
-Anchovies.

905
00:34:56,360 --> 00:34:57,829
-Broth.
-He's making broth.

906
00:34:57,895 --> 00:35:00,832
-So you can make broth with tomato?
-Pasta broth.

907
00:35:00,898 --> 00:35:02,467
-Pasta broth?
-Pasta broth.

908
00:35:03,034 --> 00:35:05,736
-Pasta broth?
-How does he come up with such ideas?

909
00:35:05,803 --> 00:35:07,138
Soju.

910
00:35:07,205 --> 00:35:08,840
-Yes.
-Soju?

911
00:35:08,906 --> 00:35:10,341
He added some soju.

912
00:35:11,843 --> 00:35:12,677
CHUGS A SHOT

913
00:35:12,743 --> 00:35:14,645
-And he had some.
-He had some too.

914
00:35:14,712 --> 00:35:15,546
WIPE

915
00:35:16,080 --> 00:35:18,516
A NEW KIND OF PERFORMANCE

916
00:35:18,850 --> 00:35:21,419
DRUNKEN BOXER FROM A FARAWAY LAND

917
00:35:21,486 --> 00:35:23,321
He's feeling a bit of stress.

918
00:35:23,387 --> 00:35:24,722
He's peeling shrimp.

919
00:35:24,789 --> 00:35:28,993
He'll grind webfoot octopus and shrimp,
then cover it with fish.

920
00:35:29,160 --> 00:35:30,695
CRACKING AN EGG

921
00:35:30,761 --> 00:35:31,596
OH!

922
00:35:32,096 --> 00:35:32,930
THROWING OUT THE YOLK?

923
00:35:32,997 --> 00:35:34,799
Why are you throwing that out, Sam?

924
00:35:34,866 --> 00:35:36,934
-Just the whites.
-Only the whites?

925
00:35:37,001 --> 00:35:39,637
-But you spilled the yolk in it?
-Yes.

926
00:35:39,704 --> 00:35:41,572
-It doesn't matter.
-Some got in.

927
00:35:41,639 --> 00:35:43,207
It's looking somewhat yellow.

928
00:35:43,274 --> 00:35:44,742
-Isn't it too thin?
-It's too thin.

929
00:35:44,809 --> 00:35:47,411
-Goodness.
-It's way too thin. What's it for?

930
00:35:47,478 --> 00:35:51,482
-What's that for?
-It's the sandwich filling.

931
00:35:51,549 --> 00:35:52,750
You need some starch.

932
00:35:52,817 --> 00:35:54,952
-Starch or flour.
-He's adding more.

933
00:35:55,753 --> 00:35:57,021
RATTLING

934
00:35:57,088 --> 00:35:58,656
-It's not grinding.
-It's not working.

935
00:35:58,723 --> 00:35:59,957
-It's not grinding.
-It's not grinding.

936
00:36:00,024 --> 00:36:02,126
-It's an emergency.
-It's an emergency.

937
00:36:02,193 --> 00:36:03,427
-It's an emergency.
-Emergency.

938
00:36:03,494 --> 00:36:05,563
-What's wrong?
-He forgot the blade!

939
00:36:05,963 --> 00:36:07,365
WHAT JUST HAPPENED?

940
00:36:07,431 --> 00:36:10,768
He forgot to attach the blade!

941
00:36:10,835 --> 00:36:13,337
It was such a basic mistake!

942
00:36:13,404 --> 00:36:14,472
He's too used to it,

943
00:36:14,539 --> 00:36:16,274
because he always removes the blades.

944
00:36:16,340 --> 00:36:17,742
EVIDENCE 1

945
00:36:17,808 --> 00:36:20,344
-He got a taste of his own medicine.
-It should've been someone else.

946
00:36:20,411 --> 00:36:22,413
He got a taste of his own medicine.

947
00:36:22,480 --> 00:36:25,249
He can't tell
if something is his or his opponent's.

948
00:36:25,583 --> 00:36:27,051
SANDWICH IT WITH FLOUNDER

949
00:36:27,118 --> 00:36:28,686
That's what the sandwich looks like.

950
00:36:29,654 --> 00:36:31,189
That's how the sandwich is made?

951
00:36:31,255 --> 00:36:34,025
That mistake was unlike him.

952
00:36:34,091 --> 00:36:35,259
He peels the shrimp.

953
00:36:35,326 --> 00:36:37,228
EDWARD USES SHRIMP TOO

954
00:36:37,795 --> 00:36:43,434
It looks like Edward
will use the shrimp as is.

955
00:36:43,501 --> 00:36:46,103
He just put a skewer into the shrimp

956
00:36:46,170 --> 00:36:49,540
and straightened its stooping back.

957
00:36:49,607 --> 00:36:51,309
I've never seen anything like it.

958
00:36:51,375 --> 00:36:52,843
He's shtraightening the shrimp.

959
00:36:52,910 --> 00:36:54,612
"Shtraightening" the shrimp?

960
00:36:55,079 --> 00:36:55,913
HIS KIND OF JOKE

961
00:36:55,980 --> 00:36:57,148
He's shtraightening the shrimp.

962
00:36:57,215 --> 00:36:59,483
-Oh, yes.
-He's shtraightening them.

963
00:36:59,550 --> 00:37:01,719
People who become a success
do have straight backs.

964
00:37:01,786 --> 00:37:03,187
So there was a reason.

965
00:37:03,254 --> 00:37:06,657
-They're never intimidated.
-No, they keep that straight.

966
00:37:06,924 --> 00:37:09,393
OIL THE PAN

967
00:37:09,727 --> 00:37:10,595
THROW IN TENDERLOIN

968
00:37:10,661 --> 00:37:12,730
He's cooking the meat,
but why is the heat so low?

969
00:37:13,497 --> 00:37:14,565
It's not on.

970
00:37:14,632 --> 00:37:16,367
It wasn't on.

971
00:37:16,434 --> 00:37:17,635
-Whoa.
-Phew.

972
00:37:17,702 --> 00:37:19,470
-Look at Sam.
-It's on fire.

973
00:37:19,537 --> 00:37:21,138
Look! It was supposed to be on.

974
00:37:21,205 --> 00:37:23,274
-Did you get confused?
-He'd turn off other people's stoves.

975
00:37:23,341 --> 00:37:26,077
-He acted out of habit, and it backfired.
-Hurry up.

976
00:37:26,143 --> 00:37:27,912
He thought he'd cook over there.

977
00:37:27,979 --> 00:37:29,447
Yes, that's it.

978
00:37:29,513 --> 00:37:30,748
-That's it.
-But it was changed.

979
00:37:31,449 --> 00:37:33,351
He thought he'd be on the right,
but he's on the left.

980
00:37:33,417 --> 00:37:35,119
-He had to move.
-Is that so?

981
00:37:35,186 --> 00:37:37,288
Everything is such a mess.

982
