WHICH CHEF WILL FINISH FIRST?

1224
00:46:32,689 --> 00:46:34,424
-Five. Four.
-Five. Four.

1225
00:46:34,491 --> 00:46:35,692
SAM KIM FINISHES ONCE AGAIN

1226
00:46:35,959 --> 00:46:36,994
DONE WITH THE POTATO TOO

1227
00:46:37,194 --> 00:46:38,662
GRABS SEAWEED BUGAK

1228
00:46:38,962 --> 00:46:40,564
-Four. Sam's done.
-Four.

1229
00:46:40,898 --> 00:46:42,566
GENEROUSLY OVER THE PASTA

1230
00:46:42,633 --> 00:46:44,434
And Edward.

1231
00:46:44,501 --> 00:46:47,304
Edward is done too.

1232
00:46:47,871 --> 00:46:49,506
-They both finished on time.
-You're the best.

1233
00:46:49,573 --> 00:46:51,608
You still have your game, Edward.

1234
00:46:52,276 --> 00:46:55,445
LUXURIOUS DISHES ARE NOW SERVED

1235
00:46:55,512 --> 00:46:56,713
He served the potato.

1236
00:46:56,980 --> 00:46:58,916
♥WITH THE POTATO♥

1237
00:46:58,982 --> 00:47:00,884
"I'll throw it in, geez."

1238
00:47:00,951 --> 00:47:02,753
"I'll throw it in, geez."

1239
00:47:03,187 --> 00:47:04,755
GREAT JOB

1240
00:47:05,823 --> 00:47:07,291
That was so quick.

1241
00:47:07,357 --> 00:47:11,895
And now, both chefs' dishes
are ready for Seon-min.

1242
00:47:11,962 --> 00:47:13,997
These dishes all seem so fancy,

1243
00:47:14,064 --> 00:47:15,766
so I am in awe.

1244
00:47:15,833 --> 00:47:19,169
It felt like I watched a magic show
for the last 15 minutes.

1245
00:47:19,236 --> 00:47:22,306
Sam finished cooking first,

1246
00:47:22,372 --> 00:47:23,907
so please try his dishes.

1247
00:47:24,575 --> 00:47:27,010
FLOUNDER SANDWICH
WITH THE FLAVOR OF SUCCESS

1248
00:47:27,277 --> 00:47:30,214
TOMATO & BEEF CARPACCIO

1249
00:47:30,480 --> 00:47:32,616
AND POTATO SLICES SERVED AS A BONUS(?)

1250
00:47:34,084 --> 00:47:36,854
CHEF SAM KIM'S THE GREAT SAMTSBY

1251
00:47:36,920 --> 00:47:39,489
Can he include
the potato in his evaluation?

1252
00:47:39,556 --> 00:47:41,792
-He can.
-He can? Okay.

1253
00:47:41,859 --> 00:47:43,994
He used some more time
to cook the potato fully.

1254
00:47:44,061 --> 00:47:48,398
First, you'll see a sauce on the side,
a mix of perilla oil and soy sauce.

1255
00:47:48,465 --> 00:47:51,869
Try the tomato carpaccio
with a pinch of that sauce over the top.

1256
00:47:51,935 --> 00:47:54,504
-That's the appetizer.
-All right.

1257
00:47:54,571 --> 00:47:57,207
That's the appetizer.

1258
00:47:57,274 --> 00:47:58,475
IN AWE♥

1259
00:47:59,309 --> 00:48:00,210
SUCK

1260
00:48:01,712 --> 00:48:03,580
-Just the soy sauce?
-Yes.

1261
00:48:03,647 --> 00:48:05,315
-All right.
-Okay.

1262
00:48:05,382 --> 00:48:06,683
A regular on ten different channels

1263
00:48:07,384 --> 00:48:10,921
is here to take titles
from legacy media programs.

1264
00:48:10,988 --> 00:48:11,955
He takes a bite.

1265
00:48:12,222 --> 00:48:13,156
I CAN TASTE SUCCESS!

1266
00:48:13,223 --> 00:48:15,058
His eyes are shut tightly.

1267
00:48:15,826 --> 00:48:18,428
I hope it makes him feel like a success.

1268
00:48:18,495 --> 00:48:21,064
-Right?
-So?

1269
00:48:21,131 --> 00:48:23,233
First, the runway is open.

1270
00:48:23,300 --> 00:48:25,569
-The runway is open?
-To fly?

1271
00:48:25,636 --> 00:48:28,906
-The runway is open.
-For you to fly straight ahead.

1272
00:48:28,972 --> 00:48:31,241
-Put it on the tomato.
-Back with the tomato.

1273
00:48:31,308 --> 00:48:33,143
-Put it in there.
-Yes.

1274
00:48:34,144 --> 00:48:36,280
He doesn't like it rare,
so it was cooked some more.

1275
00:48:37,447 --> 00:48:38,882
CHUCKLING

1276
00:48:38,949 --> 00:48:40,851
He makes it look so good.

1277
00:48:40,918 --> 00:48:42,986
He eats really well.

1278
00:48:43,053 --> 00:48:44,521
I've never had this combination before.

1279
00:48:44,588 --> 00:48:48,158
I've never thought to mix
tomatoes with <i>gamtae</i> in my life,

1280
00:48:48,225 --> 00:48:50,460
<i>but they go really well together.</i>

1281
00:48:50,527 --> 00:48:53,397
I also love perilla oil,

1282
00:48:53,463 --> 00:48:54,765
so it hit me nicely.

1283
00:48:54,831 --> 00:48:58,535
-Let me try a potato slice.
-Yes, the potato.

1284
00:48:58,602 --> 00:49:00,070
I hate wasting food.

1285
00:49:00,137 --> 00:49:02,806
Yes, so he asked to serve it to him.

1286
00:49:04,441 --> 00:49:06,143
IT'S SO GOOD

1287
00:49:06,743 --> 00:49:08,478
FEELING AWKWARD

1288
00:49:08,545 --> 00:49:10,781
-Why did you want to throw this away?
-Yes, why?

1289
00:49:10,847 --> 00:49:12,783
-See?
-Does it add to the score?

1290
00:49:12,849 --> 00:49:15,552
-It's a plus.
-It's a plus.

1291
00:49:15,619 --> 00:49:17,421
You got mad at us before.

1292
00:49:17,721 --> 00:49:19,923
HE WAS SO GRUMPY…

1293
00:49:20,324 --> 00:49:21,158
GLAD HE SERVED IT

1294
00:49:21,224 --> 00:49:22,693
We totally helped you.

1295
00:49:22,759 --> 00:49:25,729
It would've been too dry
if it got cooked a bit longer.

1296
00:49:25,796 --> 00:49:28,298
<i>-Does it have texture?</i>
<i>-Yes, it has a bit of bite to it.</i>

1297
00:49:28,365 --> 00:49:29,533
It's so delicious.

1298
00:49:29,599 --> 00:49:30,901
I see.

1299
00:49:30,968 --> 00:49:32,970
Next, the flounder dish.

1300
00:49:33,470 --> 00:49:35,238
What's inside? Shrimp and what?

1301
00:49:35,305 --> 00:49:37,207
<i>-Shrimp and webfoot octopuses.</i>
<i>-Webfoot octopus.</i>

1302
00:49:37,908 --> 00:49:40,544
Those who are successful
don't get a huge serving.

1303
00:49:40,844 --> 00:49:41,878
THE TASTE(?) OF FINE DINING

1304
00:49:41,945 --> 00:49:43,413
Please do understand.

1305
00:49:43,480 --> 00:49:46,083
-It's just two bites.
-Don't you get more if you're a success?

1306
00:49:46,149 --> 00:49:47,384
The man who succeeded.

1307
00:49:48,585 --> 00:49:49,619
That was so neat.

1308
00:49:50,620 --> 00:49:51,855
He eats with a clean bite.

1309
00:49:51,922 --> 00:49:53,256
THE 2ND FROWN

1310
00:49:53,323 --> 00:49:54,458
HEH HEH

1311
00:49:54,524 --> 00:49:55,792
He's starting to chuckle.

1312
00:49:55,859 --> 00:49:57,828
MMM♥

1313
00:49:58,061 --> 00:49:59,629
LAUGHING OUT LOUD

1314
00:49:59,696 --> 00:50:00,564
Fine dining.

1315
00:50:00,630 --> 00:50:02,199
DA-HAO(?)

1316
00:50:03,433 --> 00:50:04,935
You sounded like a rich Chinese guy.

1317
00:50:05,002 --> 00:50:06,303
<i>Hao!</i>

1318
00:50:06,903 --> 00:50:09,339
ONE MORE NICKNAME: RICH CHINESE GUY ENERGY

1319
00:50:09,406 --> 00:50:10,841
Man.

1320
00:50:10,907 --> 00:50:13,010
He's become a success only recently.

1321
00:50:13,076 --> 00:50:14,144
TWO BITES, AND IT'S DONE

1322
00:50:14,211 --> 00:50:17,647
He's sucking on his spoon too.

1323
00:50:17,714 --> 00:50:19,816
-He leaves no leftovers.
-Is it too salty?

1324
00:50:19,883 --> 00:50:22,052
It's seasoned perfectly to my taste.

1325
00:50:22,119 --> 00:50:23,720
It's so perfect.

1326
00:50:25,322 --> 00:50:26,423
I'm mesmerized.

1327
00:50:26,490 --> 00:50:27,791
What about the sauce?

1328
00:50:27,858 --> 00:50:29,626
What did you put in here?

1329
00:50:29,693 --> 00:50:32,162
<i>Vinegar, water,</i>

1330
00:50:32,229 --> 00:50:33,663
<i>and garlic.</i>

1331
00:50:33,730 --> 00:50:36,500
It tastes like
he squeezed lemon juice into this.

1332
00:50:36,566 --> 00:50:38,702
-I usually do, but you didn't have it.
-Is it tangy?

1333
00:50:38,769 --> 00:50:40,837
So I used vinegar instead.

1334
00:50:40,904 --> 00:50:42,272
It's tangy.

1335
00:50:42,339 --> 00:50:45,342
<i>I'd never have guessed</i>
<i>he had to improvise. It's so good.</i>

1336
00:50:45,409 --> 00:50:46,710
-I see.
-Yes.

1337
00:50:46,777 --> 00:50:47,811
Huh?

1338
00:50:47,878 --> 00:50:49,579
That's all. There's nothing more.

1339
00:50:49,646 --> 00:50:52,015
Successful people only eat that much.

1340
00:50:52,082 --> 00:50:54,885
Successful people
must be left wanting more.

1341
00:50:56,186 --> 00:50:58,455
-Because they eat it often.
-Because they eat it often.

1342
00:50:58,522 --> 00:50:59,923
They have lots of chances to eat it.

1343
00:50:59,990 --> 00:51:03,260
Next, Chef Edward's dish, Edward LuxuLee.

1344
00:51:03,326 --> 00:51:04,895
-LuxuLee.
-His shrimp pasta.

1345
00:51:06,396 --> 00:51:08,799
ONE AND ONLY PASTA IN THIS WORLD

1346
00:51:09,566 --> 00:51:13,537
A LUXURIOUS FEAST MADE BY
THE MAIN CHEF OF AN APEC GALA DINNER

1347
00:51:13,904 --> 00:51:17,374
CHEF EDWARD'S EDWARD LUXULEE

1348
00:51:18,675 --> 00:51:20,210
We usually eat pasta

1349
00:51:20,277 --> 00:51:22,012
with a fork, right?

1350
00:51:22,079 --> 00:51:23,513
Yes, why are you so conscious?

1351
00:51:23,580 --> 00:51:25,782
-Because I'm not used to this.
-You can use that.

1352
00:51:25,849 --> 00:51:27,117
It was hard for me too.

1353
00:51:27,617 --> 00:51:29,519
A HUGE SWOOP WITH A FORK

1354
00:51:29,586 --> 00:51:31,755
He made it with shrimp and fried abalone.

1355
00:51:32,589 --> 00:51:34,257
-He's trying the dish…
-That's a lot.

1356
00:51:34,324 --> 00:51:35,659
…made by the gala dinner's chef.

1357
00:51:37,194 --> 00:51:38,829
Is he eating <i>japchae?</i>

1358
00:51:38,895 --> 00:51:40,363
It looks like it.

1359
00:51:40,430 --> 00:51:42,833
I've never seen anyone
slurp pasta like that.

1360
00:51:43,100 --> 00:51:46,436
CHEWING

1361
00:51:46,503 --> 00:51:48,538
You chew really fast too.

1362
00:51:48,605 --> 00:51:51,241
-World leaders don't eat that fast.
-He's like a grinder.

1363
00:51:51,308 --> 00:51:54,177
-Zucchini and shrimp roll.
-He eats really quickly.

1364
00:51:54,244 --> 00:51:55,912
The RPM is insane.

1365
00:51:56,146 --> 00:51:58,915
YUM

1366
00:51:58,982 --> 00:52:00,016
Why do you eat so fast?

1367
00:52:00,083 --> 00:52:01,151
BRING UP THE RPM

1368
00:52:01,651 --> 00:52:03,587
A THUMBS-UP

1369
00:52:03,653 --> 00:52:04,554
All right.

1370
00:52:04,621 --> 00:52:07,724
He suddenly
turned into a rich Chinese guy.

1371
00:52:07,791 --> 00:52:08,959
He's like a client.

1372
00:52:09,025 --> 00:52:10,093
FULL OF CHINESE VIBES…

1373
00:52:10,494 --> 00:52:12,362
SWITCHES TOOLS AND GOES BACK

1374
00:52:12,429 --> 00:52:15,098
-It looks like he's having <i>japchae.</i>
-It does.

1375
00:52:15,165 --> 00:52:18,468
-That's <i>japchae.</i>
-It's <i>japchae.</i>

1376
00:52:18,535 --> 00:52:20,971
THE DISHWASHER IS AT WORK

1377
00:52:21,037 --> 00:52:23,140
-It's the pasta he learned…
-That's more like it.

1378
00:52:23,206 --> 00:52:24,808
…from an older lady in southern France.

1379
00:52:24,875 --> 00:52:26,476
It tastes

1380
00:52:26,543 --> 00:52:28,578
more Korean than French.

1381
00:52:28,645 --> 00:52:30,247
-Is that so?
-Yes.

1382
00:52:30,313 --> 00:52:32,949
-He used kimchi juice at the end, right?
-Yes.

1383
00:52:33,583 --> 00:52:34,851
ADDING A TOUCH OF KOREA

1384
00:52:34,918 --> 00:52:38,121
<i>He also used </i>bugak <i>and </i>ramyeon <i>soup base.</i>

1385
00:52:38,188 --> 00:52:39,956
Man.

1386
00:52:40,023 --> 00:52:42,259
-He's drunk on success.
-Yes, he's drunk on success.

1387
00:52:42,325 --> 00:52:44,127
-It's really good.
-Is it?

1388
00:52:44,194 --> 00:52:45,829
It's really good.

1389
00:52:45,896 --> 00:52:47,797
He rolled shrimp with zucchini, didn't he?

1390
00:52:47,864 --> 00:52:49,733
-Yes.
-What's that like?

1391
00:52:50,734 --> 00:52:52,669
JUST THE ROLL!

1392
00:52:54,237 --> 00:52:55,405
It tastes like

1393
00:52:56,239 --> 00:52:58,475
a shrimp wrapped with zucchini.

1394
00:52:59,809 --> 00:53:00,877
-That's it.
-We know that.

1395
00:53:00,944 --> 00:53:03,780
But the thing is,
I personally love zucchinis,

1396
00:53:03,847 --> 00:53:05,782
<i>and because it was so thinly sliced,</i>

1397
00:53:05,849 --> 00:53:08,018
<i>it felt only as thin as shrimp peels,</i>

1398
00:53:08,084 --> 00:53:09,986
<i>making two ingredients seem like one.</i>

1399
00:53:10,587 --> 00:53:11,488
And the abalone?

1400
00:53:11,555 --> 00:53:13,290
-It's there.
-Is this it?

1401
00:53:13,356 --> 00:53:14,691
-Abalone.
-That's it.

1402
00:53:15,292 --> 00:53:16,126
Fried abalone.

1403
00:53:17,394 --> 00:53:19,429
BRAVO

1404
00:53:19,496 --> 00:53:21,631
The batter is stuck right on to it.

1405
00:53:21,698 --> 00:53:23,967
I don't think I've had
such delicious abalone before.

1406
00:53:24,034 --> 00:53:25,902
Can you taste your success?

1407
00:53:25,969 --> 00:53:27,404
It tastes like success itself.

1408
00:53:27,470 --> 00:53:29,906
Even if I saw 100,000 won here,
I wouldn't pick it up.

1409
00:53:30,540 --> 00:53:33,210
-I get it. He doesn't care.
-He doesn't even care.

1410
00:53:33,276 --> 00:53:35,946
All I want to do is enjoy this pasta.

1411
00:53:36,012 --> 00:53:37,380
How about a million won?

1412
00:53:37,981 --> 00:53:39,683
I'll get up for a second, pick it up,

1413
00:53:39,749 --> 00:53:41,718
then come back to
the slightly cooled-down pasta.

1414
00:53:41,785 --> 00:53:42,852
Slightly cooled down?

1415
00:53:42,919 --> 00:53:45,655
-It's that delicious.
-But you're here to eat this.

1416
00:53:46,656 --> 00:53:48,525
He's devouring the pasta.

1417
00:53:48,592 --> 00:53:51,428
It feels like Sam's dishes
are slowly getting forgotten.

1418
00:53:51,795 --> 00:53:52,629
REACHING OUT

1419
00:53:53,563 --> 00:53:55,165
You must like potatoes.

1420
00:53:55,232 --> 00:53:57,000
I have to compare the two.

1421
00:53:57,067 --> 00:53:59,703
-What if he didn't serve this?
-While he eats, COKUN.

1422
00:53:59,769 --> 00:54:02,005
Can you give some to COKUN?

1423
00:54:02,072 --> 00:54:04,741
Only COKUN will try the dishes.

1424
00:54:04,808 --> 00:54:07,244
-With the dressing.
-And mix them.

1425
00:54:07,310 --> 00:54:08,144
HEHE

1426
00:54:08,211 --> 00:54:10,680
HE'S ALREADY SMILING?

1427
00:54:10,747 --> 00:54:12,515
-Mmm.
-He's already smiling.

1428
00:54:12,582 --> 00:54:14,951
You can't hide it on your face.

1429
00:54:15,018 --> 00:54:17,554
I think I've only eaten
bad food to this day.

1430
00:54:17,887 --> 00:54:19,489
OVERCOMING HIS SMALL APPETITE(?)

1431
00:54:19,556 --> 00:54:22,826
I want to eat this
in the morning from now on.

1432
00:54:23,159 --> 00:54:24,761
THE LIGHT EATER'S NEW BREAKFAST

1433
00:54:24,828 --> 00:54:26,062
Next, flounder.

1434
00:54:26,129 --> 00:54:28,164
I've been curious about this one.

1435
00:54:28,231 --> 00:54:29,833
Gosh.

1436
00:54:30,467 --> 00:54:31,468
This is mine.

1437
00:54:31,901 --> 00:54:33,003
WARY OF THE DISHWASHER

1438
00:54:33,470 --> 00:54:35,572
The other guest usually craves the food.

1439
00:54:35,639 --> 00:54:36,806
This can't taste bad.

1440
00:54:36,873 --> 00:54:39,042
-He even ate the potato.
-He eats well.

1441
00:54:41,011 --> 00:54:43,146
ANOTHER BIG SMILE

1442
00:54:44,447 --> 00:54:46,316
It tastes even better

1443
00:54:46,383 --> 00:54:49,019
than the fresh fish I bought
to satisfy my craving.

1444
00:54:49,085 --> 00:54:50,053
Obviously though.

1445
00:54:50,120 --> 00:54:52,522
He has changed it completely.

1446
00:54:52,589 --> 00:54:55,892
-He finished everything.
-What about the next episode?

1447
00:54:55,959 --> 00:54:57,560
I'll starve for two weeks after.

1448
00:54:57,961 --> 00:54:59,462
GOT ENOUGH NUTRIENTS FOR 2 WEEKS

1449
00:54:59,529 --> 00:55:02,265
Next, Edward LuxuLee.

1450
00:55:02,332 --> 00:55:05,568
-He used kimchi juice.
-Right?

1451
00:55:05,635 --> 00:55:08,672
It almost looks like <i>bibim-guksu,</i>
spicy mixed noodles.

1452
00:55:08,738 --> 00:55:11,241
-He's…
-Mmm.

1453
00:55:11,741 --> 00:55:12,909
WHAT DO YOU THINK?

1454
00:55:12,976 --> 00:55:14,577
This reminds me

1455
00:55:14,644 --> 00:55:16,112
of my grandmother.

1456
00:55:16,179 --> 00:55:19,282
-Does it taste like your grandma's recipe?
-If my grandma

1457
00:55:19,349 --> 00:55:22,352
learned to make pasta
as I like it, it'd taste like this.

1458
00:55:24,521 --> 00:55:25,555
Because of kimchi.

1459
00:55:25,622 --> 00:55:28,291
-It tastes like that.
-The kimchi juice is the kick.

1460
00:55:28,358 --> 00:55:30,393
-I see.
-That flavor makes it delicious.

1461
00:55:30,460 --> 00:55:33,630
Chef Edward, where did you get the idea
to add kimchi juice?

1462
00:55:33,697 --> 00:55:36,199
Because he didn't have lemons.

1463
00:55:36,266 --> 00:55:38,234
-Because of the sourness.
-To add sourness.

1464
00:55:38,301 --> 00:55:40,036
-I see.
-It was to add sourness.

1465
00:55:40,103 --> 00:55:42,706
And that sour note
is what keeps making me go back.

1466
00:55:43,239 --> 00:55:46,276
HE CONTINUES TO EAT TOO

1467
00:55:46,976 --> 00:55:49,012
THE DISHWASHER IS IN FULL THROTTLE

1468
00:55:49,079 --> 00:55:51,214
Seon-min, you have to start thinking.

1469
00:55:51,281 --> 00:55:53,483
How did he make this in 15 minutes?

1470
00:55:54,984 --> 00:55:56,553
He finishes the pasta too.

1471
00:55:56,619 --> 00:55:57,721
Seon-min scrapes everything.

1472
00:55:57,787 --> 00:55:59,389
He finished everything.

1473
00:55:59,456 --> 00:56:02,292
-And the potato.
-He's eating them all.

1474
00:56:04,094 --> 00:56:04,928
COLLECTING ALL THE FOOD

1475
00:56:04,994 --> 00:56:07,197
All in one bowl.

1476
00:56:08,131 --> 00:56:10,166
It ended up looking like <i>gukbap.</i>

1477
00:56:11,801 --> 00:56:14,504
WIPING EVERYTHING OFF

1478
00:56:14,571 --> 00:56:15,438
Gosh.

1479
00:56:15,505 --> 00:56:17,707
I'm glad that he likes it.

1480
00:56:18,641 --> 00:56:20,710
FINISHES WASHING THIS ONE TOO

1481
00:56:20,777 --> 00:56:22,412
He makes it look delicious.

1482
00:56:22,479 --> 00:56:24,314
I heard you already had
a box meal before we started.

1483
00:56:24,381 --> 00:56:25,949
-What?
-Are you serious?

1484
00:56:26,015 --> 00:56:29,619
-You're something.
-He got hungry and had to eat.

1485
00:56:29,686 --> 00:56:31,121
I want to finish everything,

1486
00:56:31,187 --> 00:56:33,656
but I'm afraid
I won't be able to eat my food.

1487
00:56:33,723 --> 00:56:35,625
-Wise decision.
-For the next episode.

1488
00:56:35,692 --> 00:56:36,760
For the next episode.

1489
00:56:36,826 --> 00:56:38,094
THE REST GOES TO THE TUBBY TWINS

1490
00:56:38,161 --> 00:56:40,096
It smells good.
It doesn't smell like kimchi.

1491
00:56:40,497 --> 00:56:41,564
SLURP

1492
00:56:43,299 --> 00:56:46,503
THE ORIGINAL DISHWASHER IS ON

1493
00:56:47,070 --> 00:56:48,071
Man.

1494
00:56:48,805 --> 00:56:49,973
It's quite tasty.

1495
00:56:51,908 --> 00:56:54,010
KIM POONG SLURPS TOO

1496
00:56:55,378 --> 00:56:56,679
Isn't it good?

1497
00:56:56,746 --> 00:57:02,519
It feels like it was made
with rich and buttery kimchi stir-fry.

1498
00:57:02,585 --> 00:57:05,054
Even kids would love this.

1499
00:57:05,121 --> 00:57:07,624
Other than the kimchi flavor,

1500
00:57:07,690 --> 00:57:10,226
-the pasta was largely French.
-Butter pasta.

1501
00:57:10,293 --> 00:57:14,030
It felt like he threw in
one Korean kick into that pasta.

1502
00:57:14,097 --> 00:57:17,767
And now, Seon-min's time to choose.

1503
00:57:17,834 --> 00:57:20,136
<i>Press one to pick Chef Edward,</i>

1504
00:57:20,203 --> 00:57:21,871
<i>and press two for Chef Sam.</i>

1505
00:57:21,938 --> 00:57:24,507
Make your decision and press the button.

1506
00:57:26,075 --> 00:57:28,745
-Did you press one?
-Yes.

1507
00:57:28,812 --> 00:57:31,247
Let's find out the result. Show us!

1508
00:57:31,848 --> 00:57:33,383
The taste of success.

1509
00:57:33,450 --> 00:57:36,953
This was the first cook-off
between Sam and Edward.

1510
00:57:37,020 --> 00:57:39,756
Will Sam break his losing streak?

1511
00:57:39,823 --> 00:57:42,158
Or will Edward get his first win?

1512
00:57:42,659 --> 00:57:45,261
Who did Seon-min pick?

1513
00:57:45,929 --> 00:57:46,863
Which one

1514
00:57:47,664 --> 00:57:48,498
is the winner?

1515
00:57:48,665 --> 00:57:50,200
SAM KIM WINS

1516
00:57:50,600 --> 00:57:51,534
THE GREAT SAMTSBY WINS

1517
00:57:51,601 --> 00:57:53,036
Sam Kim!

1518
00:57:53,603 --> 00:57:55,805
Sam Kim wins!

1519
00:57:55,872 --> 00:57:58,107
He finally ends
his three-time losing streak.

1520
00:57:58,174 --> 00:58:01,010
It was really hard to decide
which one I liked better.

1521
00:58:01,077 --> 00:58:04,981
The <i>gamtae</i> and tomato appetizer

1522
00:58:05,048 --> 00:58:07,317
opened up the runway for me,

1523
00:58:07,383 --> 00:58:10,019
and the potato became

1524
00:58:10,086 --> 00:58:11,788
such a huge plus.

1525
00:58:11,855 --> 00:58:13,456
-Really?
-The potato!

1526
00:58:13,523 --> 00:58:15,225
-You needed them!
-He was like, "Geez."

1527
00:58:15,291 --> 00:58:19,863
-I was going to serve them.
-Why did he try to throw them out?

1528
00:58:19,929 --> 00:58:22,398
When we said you should serve the potato,

1529
00:58:22,465 --> 00:58:24,334
-You snapped.
-…you showed us your canine.

1530
00:58:24,400 --> 00:58:25,602
We deserve credit.

1531
00:58:25,668 --> 00:58:29,639
Well, I was a little mad
when you told me to serve the potato,

1532
00:58:29,706 --> 00:58:31,007
but now that I've seen the result,

1533
00:58:31,074 --> 00:58:32,609
-yes, it was helpful.
-See?

1534
00:58:32,675 --> 00:58:34,244
-See?
-We told you.

1535
00:58:34,310 --> 00:58:37,547
Please put a star on Chef Sam.

1536
00:58:37,614 --> 00:58:39,616
This is his ninth victory,

1537
00:58:39,682 --> 00:58:41,885
and he takes his ninth star.

1538
00:58:42,452 --> 00:58:44,420
He finally gets a star after three losses.

1539
00:58:44,487 --> 00:58:45,388
Congrats!

1540
00:58:45,889 --> 00:58:50,293
<i>The second cook-off</i>
<i>played with Seon-min's ingredients.</i>

1541
00:58:50,360 --> 00:58:51,961
The leftover squad

1542
00:58:52,028 --> 00:58:54,564
and the dishwasher of the show,
Chef Yoon Nam-no,

1543
00:58:54,631 --> 00:58:57,033
versus someone who makes
her enemy's head spin,

1544
00:58:57,100 --> 00:59:00,503
the spin-dryer of the human psyche,
Chef Park Eun-yeong.

1545
00:59:00,570 --> 00:59:01,571
"The spin-dryer"?

1546
00:59:01,638 --> 00:59:04,173
You two had four rounds this season

1547
00:59:04,240 --> 00:59:06,009
and both won twice and lost twice.

1548
00:59:06,075 --> 00:59:08,978
-They're neck-and-neck.
-Yes, neck-and-neck.

1549
00:59:09,045 --> 00:59:12,115
And they're both on a roll right now.

1550
00:59:12,181 --> 00:59:13,816
Eun-yeong has won twice in a row,

1551
00:59:13,883 --> 00:59:16,419
-and Nam-no, three times in a row.
-How did that happen?

1552
00:59:16,486 --> 00:59:20,023
Isn't this order
way too advantageous for Nam-no?

1553
00:59:20,089 --> 00:59:21,124
-Exactly.
-Correct.

1554
00:59:21,190 --> 00:59:26,095
When you order fruit at a pub,
it comes with parsley garnish.

1555
00:59:26,162 --> 00:59:27,830
I even eat those.

1556
00:59:28,064 --> 00:59:28,898
IS HE INTRIGUED?

1557
00:59:28,965 --> 00:59:30,233
Most people don't eat that.

1558
00:59:30,300 --> 00:59:31,134
Do you eat the parsley too?

1559
00:59:31,200 --> 00:59:33,236
-Of course.
-Okay.

1560
00:59:33,303 --> 00:59:35,772
<i>Hoe, </i>raw fish fillets, are served
on a bed of shredded radish, right?

1561
00:59:35,838 --> 00:59:37,173
-The radish.
-He wouldn't.

1562
00:59:37,240 --> 00:59:38,942
That stuff is scrumptious.

1563
00:59:39,008 --> 00:59:40,343
I always eat it.

1564
00:59:40,410 --> 00:59:42,378
-It's delicious with soy sauce.
-Also,

1565
00:59:42,445 --> 00:59:45,582
people say you end up in a bad place
if you leave too much leftovers.

1566
00:59:45,648 --> 00:59:48,151
But I'd have
nothing to eat in the bad place.

1567
00:59:48,217 --> 00:59:49,752
I've been eating it all on this side.

1568
00:59:49,986 --> 00:59:52,589
MOVED

1569
00:59:52,655 --> 00:59:55,224
-You'll starve.
-You can go on a diet.

1570
00:59:55,291 --> 00:59:57,527
I leave my food to eat there.

1571
00:59:57,594 --> 01:00:00,096
-But you are going to the bad place?
-He's going there.

1572
01:00:00,163 --> 01:00:02,865
That's the worst place for him.
Starving down there.

1573
01:00:02,932 --> 01:00:04,200
You're worried about the afterlife?

1574
01:00:05,234 --> 01:00:08,204
Eun-yeong, isn't this
disadvantageous for you?

1575
01:00:08,271 --> 01:00:10,173
You may think so,

1576
01:00:10,239 --> 01:00:12,442
but Yoon Nam-no…

1577
01:00:12,508 --> 01:00:14,577
-"Yoon Nam-no"?
-I mean, Chef Nam-no.

1578
01:00:14,644 --> 01:00:16,179
She doesn't even call him a chef.

1579
01:00:16,245 --> 01:00:18,548
-And she's a year younger.
-Do you want to win that badly?

1580
01:00:18,615 --> 01:00:19,782
He's still older.

1581
01:00:19,849 --> 01:00:22,051
I must be obsessed with the star. Sorry.

1582
01:00:22,118 --> 01:00:24,253
There's no order under this roof.

1583
01:00:24,320 --> 01:00:25,888
HAPPY CHEF & MY FRIDGE FAMILY

1584
01:00:25,955 --> 01:00:27,323
-Let me try that again.
-He's older too.

1585
01:00:27,390 --> 01:00:30,793
Chef Nam-no does have
the dishwasher energy,

1586
01:00:30,860 --> 01:00:32,228
which means he's just busy eating.

1587
01:00:32,295 --> 01:00:34,230
-Ah-ha.
-Instead of tasting the food,

1588
01:00:34,297 --> 01:00:36,432
-he's just busy stuffing himself.
-True.

1589
01:00:36,499 --> 01:00:38,735
So someone with a small appetite,

1590
01:00:38,801 --> 01:00:40,370
like me, would know

1591
01:00:40,436 --> 01:00:42,872
what to make
to let you enjoy your food to the end.

1592
01:00:42,939 --> 01:00:47,510
I think I can make a dish you'll devour
and enjoy until your last bite.

1593
01:00:47,577 --> 01:00:49,345
Nam-no only stuffs his face.

1594
01:00:50,279 --> 01:00:52,281
CHEF NAM-NO'S INGREDIENTS:
TENDERLOIN, STRIP LOIN, BLACK LENTIL

1595
01:00:52,348 --> 01:00:54,350
BARLEY, DRIED SHINGLED HEDGEHOG,
PICKLED VICTORY ONION, RADISH, AND MORE

1596
01:00:54,417 --> 01:00:57,553
Nam-no, what are you making
to win against her?

1597
01:00:57,620 --> 01:00:59,088
The dish I'm making is called

1598
01:01:00,089 --> 01:01:02,759
Did You Have to Scrape

1599
01:01:02,825 --> 01:01:04,761
the Bowl Clean?

1600
01:01:05,862 --> 01:01:07,664
<i>-Sunflower.</i>
<i>-Sunflower.</i>

1601
01:01:07,730 --> 01:01:09,899
-That was scary.
-I remember.

1602
01:01:09,966 --> 01:01:12,602
-What is it?
-First of all,

1603
01:01:12,669 --> 01:01:14,037
I'll make <i>jjol-galbi.</i>

1604
01:01:14,103 --> 01:01:16,773
<i>-Jjol-galbi?</i>
-Meat simmered in sauce?

1605
01:01:16,839 --> 01:01:18,908
And a perfect pair with <i>jjol-galbi,</i>

1606
01:01:18,975 --> 01:01:20,710
<i>mu-saengchae, </i>spicy radish salad.

1607
01:01:20,777 --> 01:01:23,680
You mentioned that you loved kelp,

1608
01:01:23,746 --> 01:01:25,815
so I'm going to mix some of that too.

1609
01:01:25,882 --> 01:01:27,250
The third dish

1610
01:01:27,316 --> 01:01:28,651
is what you'll love, hot pot rice.

1611
01:01:29,519 --> 01:01:31,788
You didn't seem to eat just white rice,

1612
01:01:31,854 --> 01:01:35,391
so I'll mix in other grains
and even black lentils.

1613
01:01:35,458 --> 01:01:39,162
The last one is the most important.
When you get scorched rice, <i>nurungji,</i>

1614
01:01:39,228 --> 01:01:40,963
you pour hot water into it
to make thin soup.

1615
01:01:41,030 --> 01:01:43,900
-Is that it?
-But I will pour my secret broth

1616
01:01:43,966 --> 01:01:46,769
and serve the most delicious <i>sungnyung,</i>
scorched rice soup,

1617
01:01:46,836 --> 01:01:49,372
made with <i>nurungji.</i>

1618
01:01:50,306 --> 01:01:52,308
CHEF EUN-YEONG'S INGREDIENTS:

1619
01:01:52,375 --> 01:01:54,377
FLOUNDER, INSTANT DUMPLING,
SIRAEGI, OYSTER SAUCE

1620
01:01:54,444 --> 01:01:56,946
What are you making, Chef Eun-yeong?

1621
01:01:57,013 --> 01:02:00,883
I'm going to use flounder
and make something crazily delicious.

1622
01:02:00,950 --> 01:02:03,386
There's a Chinese dish
called <i>hong shao yu.</i>

1623
01:02:03,453 --> 01:02:06,289
I'm going to make that in Cantonese style,

1624
01:02:06,355 --> 01:02:08,458
by deep-frying and braising the fish.

1625
01:02:08,524 --> 01:02:10,493
I'll also make wheat crêpe

1626
01:02:10,560 --> 01:02:12,695
and help you scrape off
every drop of sauce,

1627
01:02:12,762 --> 01:02:15,031
-turning you into a dishwasher.
-Even crêpe?

1628
01:02:15,198 --> 01:02:16,933
MOUTH-WATERING

1629
01:02:16,999 --> 01:02:18,534
And now, let us begin the cook-off

1630
01:02:18,601 --> 01:02:22,338
with Seon-min's next order,
Dishwasher Energy.

1631
01:02:22,405 --> 01:02:23,706
Fight me, Eun-yeong.

1632
01:02:25,007 --> 01:02:26,142
Come at me.

1633
01:02:28,044 --> 01:02:29,278
Eun-yeong is younger than him,

1634
01:02:29,345 --> 01:02:31,714
but she's still doing this.

1635
01:02:31,781 --> 01:02:32,715
She's like Chun-Li.

1636
01:02:32,782 --> 01:02:34,350
Chun-Li. Chun-Li and…

1637
01:02:34,417 --> 01:02:36,319
IT'S LIKE AN OLD FIGHTING GAME

1638
01:02:36,385 --> 01:02:39,555
Here to rub his victory on her face
as the show's dishwasher,

1639
01:02:39,622 --> 01:02:40,990
Chef Yoon Nam-no.

1640
01:02:41,057 --> 01:02:44,193
Here to spin-dry his psyche today,

1641
01:02:44,260 --> 01:02:46,763
the show's spin-dryer,
Chef Park Eun-yeong.

1642
01:02:46,829 --> 01:02:50,700
Which one of them will satisfy
the guy with the dishwasher energy?

1643
01:02:50,767 --> 01:02:53,102
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

1644
01:02:54,570 --> 01:02:57,573
It'll be Chinese cuisine
versus Korean cuisine.

1645
01:02:57,640 --> 01:02:58,508
BEGINS WITH A STONE POT

1646
01:02:58,574 --> 01:03:02,145
Nam-no has to start the rice first.
There's not a lot of time.

1647
01:03:02,211 --> 01:03:05,681
She's going to fry the flounder
and then braise it,

1648
01:03:05,748 --> 01:03:07,917
-so that'll take a while.
-It will.

1649
01:03:07,984 --> 01:03:11,220
Chef Eun-yeong is making
Cantonese-style braised fish.

1650
01:03:12,088 --> 01:03:13,089
Is that kelp water?

1651
01:03:13,156 --> 01:03:15,558
-Whoa.
-No, it's not kelp.

1652
01:03:15,625 --> 01:03:16,759
It's shingled hedgehog water.

1653
01:03:16,826 --> 01:03:18,227
-Shingled hedgehog?
-What?

1654
01:03:18,294 --> 01:03:19,495
It's already…

1655
01:03:19,562 --> 01:03:20,530
That's going to be good.

1656
01:03:20,596 --> 01:03:21,964
That's guaranteed to be good.

1657
01:03:22,532 --> 01:03:24,233
I thought those were
the cherries from before.

1658
01:03:25,668 --> 01:03:26,869
How's he going to use cherry juice?

1659
01:03:27,136 --> 01:03:28,371
CHERRY JUICE PORRIDGE

1660
01:03:29,238 --> 01:03:31,274
-He added pickled victory onion.
-Pickled victory onion?

1661
01:03:31,340 --> 01:03:32,742
-Pickled victory onion?
-Pickled victory onion.

1662
01:03:32,809 --> 01:03:34,811
Nam-no's working hard on the hot pot rice.

1663
01:03:34,877 --> 01:03:37,013
-Shingled hedgehog.
-That's an old dude's appetite.

1664
01:03:37,079 --> 01:03:39,782
-They both found lots of stuff in there.
-They did.

1665
01:03:39,849 --> 01:03:41,617
He has quite a few ingredients.

1666
01:03:41,684 --> 01:03:43,786
This rice is almost medicinal.

1667
01:03:43,853 --> 01:03:45,254
The rice alone would taste great.

1668
01:03:45,721 --> 01:03:46,689
PREPPED FLOUNDER

1669
01:03:46,956 --> 01:03:48,758
INTO THE OIL!

1670
01:03:49,358 --> 01:03:50,193
SURPRISED

1671
01:03:50,259 --> 01:03:53,663
-It's too hot.
-She seasoned the flounder first.

1672
01:03:53,729 --> 01:03:55,364
Coat with soy sauce, then deep-fry it.

1673
01:03:55,431 --> 01:03:56,732
She's sticking it in.

1674
01:03:56,799 --> 01:03:58,901
Isn't the oil too hot?

1675
01:03:58,968 --> 01:04:01,571
Oh, no. The oil seems too hot.

1676
01:04:01,971 --> 01:04:03,673
EVERYONE IS IN SHOCK

1677
01:04:03,739 --> 01:04:06,142
BUT THE STEADY CHEF REMAINS CALM

1678
01:04:06,209 --> 01:04:07,643
She's not flustered.

1679
01:04:07,710 --> 01:04:10,780
-That's too much oil.
-It's overflowing.

1680
01:04:10,847 --> 01:04:13,249
Do you have
to raise the temperature this high?

1681
01:04:13,316 --> 01:04:14,684
I don't have much time,

1682
01:04:14,750 --> 01:04:17,820
so I need to fry this until crispy
to let it make better broth.

1683
01:04:18,955 --> 01:04:20,056
She had a reason.

1684
01:04:20,122 --> 01:04:21,023
I turned the gas off.

1685
01:04:21,090 --> 01:04:22,391
FLAPPING

1686
01:04:22,458 --> 01:04:24,694
-Calm down.
-Stay calm.

1687
01:04:24,760 --> 01:04:27,096
That flounder is almost alive.

1688
01:04:28,197 --> 01:04:32,101
The flounder has been there for a while,
but you've all managed to make it fresh.

1689
01:04:32,869 --> 01:04:34,971
NAM-NO WILL USE SHREDDED DRIED POLLACK

1690
01:04:35,271 --> 01:04:36,572
ONION

1691
01:04:36,939 --> 01:04:39,942
3. BOIL DRIED POLLACK AND ONION
TO MAKE POLLACK BROTH

1692
01:04:40,943 --> 01:04:42,979
Wait, salt?

1693
01:04:43,045 --> 01:04:44,313
This? No.

1694
01:04:44,380 --> 01:04:46,082
-Is it for the radish salad?
-No.

1695
01:04:46,148 --> 01:04:48,084
One second. I can't multitask.

1696
01:04:48,150 --> 01:04:49,318
-What is it?
-What is it?

1697
01:04:49,385 --> 01:04:51,621
-It's <i>jjol-galbi</i> sauce.
<i>-Jjol-galbi.</i>

1698
01:04:51,687 --> 01:04:52,588
Isn't that too much water?

1699
01:04:52,655 --> 01:04:53,589
And gochujang.

1700
01:04:53,656 --> 01:04:55,892
4. MIX SUGAR, OLIGOSACCHARIDE SYRUP,
SOY SAUCE, GOCHUJANG

1701
01:04:55,958 --> 01:04:57,159
Radish slices.

1702
01:04:57,860 --> 01:04:59,428
Watch your hand.

1703
01:05:00,496 --> 01:05:01,898
He's making really thin pieces.

1704
01:05:03,199 --> 01:05:05,201
RED PEPPER POWDER, SUGAR, TUNA SAUCE

1705
01:05:05,268 --> 01:05:07,203
-What is it?
<i>-Mu-saengchae, </i>spicy radish salad.

1706
01:05:07,270 --> 01:05:08,537
-Radish salad?
-He's making radish salad.

1707
01:05:08,604 --> 01:05:10,406
Is he cutting kelp into thin pieces?

1708
01:05:10,473 --> 01:05:12,875
-Do you usually add kelp to that?
-I'm using kelp.

1709
01:05:12,942 --> 01:05:14,510
-Kelp sounds good.
-That's new.

1710
01:05:14,577 --> 01:05:16,145
Won't it become sticky?

1711
01:05:16,212 --> 01:05:17,246
Even better.

1712
01:05:17,313 --> 01:05:18,414
It'll stick to your taste buds.

1713
01:05:18,481 --> 01:05:21,517
-You're doing that on purpose?
-He threw in some kelp.

1714
01:05:21,584 --> 01:05:23,319
I love radish salad,

1715
01:05:23,386 --> 01:05:26,222
but I've never had it with kelp.

1716
01:05:26,289 --> 01:05:27,790
There must be a reason
why people don't use it.

1717
01:05:29,058 --> 01:05:29,892
"THAT'S ORIGINAL," BUT RUDELY

1718
01:05:29,959 --> 01:05:31,494
Ho-young states calmly

1719
01:05:31,560 --> 01:05:33,629
with an innocent look on his face.

1720
01:05:33,696 --> 01:05:35,498
"There must be a reason
why people don't use it."

1721
01:05:35,564 --> 01:05:38,334
-There must be a reason why…
-His face was so innocent.

1722
01:05:39,902 --> 01:05:40,803
LOL

1723
01:05:40,870 --> 01:05:43,839
-Eun-yeong gets ready to make chili oil.
-Chili oil.

1724
01:05:44,507 --> 01:05:45,841
That looks so nice.

1725
01:05:46,609 --> 01:05:49,011
I also love spicy food.

1726
01:05:49,078 --> 01:05:51,714
But nothing too spicy.

1727
01:05:51,781 --> 01:05:53,582
Make it tasty yet savory.

1728
01:05:53,649 --> 01:05:54,550
Is that <i>tteok</i>, rice cake?

1729
01:05:54,617 --> 01:05:56,452
-Green onion?
-Right. Green onion.

1730
01:05:57,153 --> 01:05:59,322
4. COOK GREEN ONION, GARLIC IN CHILI OIL,
THEN BRAISE THE FISH

1731
01:05:59,388 --> 01:06:03,092
She first deep-fried the fish,
and is now braising it.

1732
01:06:03,159 --> 01:06:04,794
-What's next?
<i>-Siraegi.</i>

1733
01:06:04,860 --> 01:06:06,362
<i>-Siraegi.</i>
<i>-Siraegi.</i>

1734
01:06:06,429 --> 01:06:08,831
She squeezed out the water.

1735
01:06:08,898 --> 01:06:13,736
It'll taste great with <i>siraegi.</i>
It already has umami in it.

1736
01:06:13,803 --> 01:06:16,439
Chef Eun-yeong,
is that Cantonese-style braised fish?

1737
01:06:16,505 --> 01:06:17,907
Yes, it's Cantonese-style.

1738
01:06:17,974 --> 01:06:19,875
But I added some chili oil
to make it a bit spicier.

1739
01:06:19,942 --> 01:06:21,677
I see.

1740
01:06:22,144 --> 01:06:23,746
MEANWHILE, THE RADISH SALAD

1741
01:06:23,813 --> 01:06:24,647
SLURP

1742
01:06:25,214 --> 01:06:26,282
Gosh.

1743
01:06:26,949 --> 01:06:27,783
MINCED GARLIC

1744
01:06:28,651 --> 01:06:29,485
LOOKS

1745
01:06:29,719 --> 01:06:30,920
CHOMP CHOMP

1746
01:06:30,987 --> 01:06:32,521
-Just a bit.
-He ate it all.

1747
01:06:32,588 --> 01:06:34,323
-Yes.
-He's eating it all while cooking.

1748
01:06:35,391 --> 01:06:37,560
-He won't have anything left.
-He's usually like that.

1749
01:06:37,626 --> 01:06:39,695
-"Usually like that."
-Nothing left?

1750
01:06:39,762 --> 01:06:41,030
KELP AND SPICY RADISH SALAD IS DONE

1751
01:06:41,097 --> 01:06:42,631
He's done with the radish salad.

1752
01:06:42,698 --> 01:06:44,400
Are you making <i>jjol-galbi </i>later?

1753
01:06:44,467 --> 01:06:46,736
-Where's the <i>galbi?</i>
-Shouldn't you start?

1754
01:06:46,802 --> 01:06:47,903
When will you boil it down?

1755
01:06:47,970 --> 01:06:50,906
-I forgot.
-You forgot? You have to hurry!

1756
01:06:50,973 --> 01:06:52,341
-Hurry up.
-What are you doing?

1757
01:06:52,408 --> 01:06:53,376
Boil it down slowly.

1758
01:06:53,442 --> 01:06:55,811
-You have ten minutes.
-You lost five minutes.

1759
01:06:55,878 --> 01:06:57,380
Get it together.

1760
01:06:57,446 --> 01:06:58,614
Why would you open that?

1761
01:06:58,681 --> 01:07:00,249
-You just closed it.
-It won't boil.

1762
01:07:00,316 --> 01:07:02,518
-It won't?
-Emergency.

1763
01:07:02,585 --> 01:07:03,686
Why won't it boil?

1764
01:07:03,753 --> 01:07:05,721
Because the stone is pretty thick.

1765
01:07:05,788 --> 01:07:08,391
-You should've started with that.
-It'll take a lot of time.

1766
01:07:08,457 --> 01:07:11,027
-It's been five minutes.
-You have no time.

1767
01:07:11,093 --> 01:07:14,397
Chef Eun-yeong is making wheat crêpes.

1768
01:07:14,463 --> 01:07:15,598
-Wheat crêpes.
-Wheat crêpes.

1769
01:07:15,664 --> 01:07:18,467
-It's her go-to recipe.
-Eun-yeong's go-to recipe, wheat crêpes.

1770
01:07:18,534 --> 01:07:21,971
We'd usually make it with a blender,

1771
01:07:22,038 --> 01:07:24,807
but she's doing everything by hand.

1772
01:07:25,074 --> 01:07:26,375
I'LL USE A BLENDER!

1773
01:07:26,442 --> 01:07:28,110
He's blending that.

1774
01:07:28,177 --> 01:07:29,545
JJOL-GALBI SAUCE

1775
01:07:29,612 --> 01:07:31,814
That looks like coffee.

1776
01:07:33,015 --> 01:07:34,283
Instant coffee.

1777
01:07:34,350 --> 01:07:36,352
You'll have to hurry.

1778
01:07:37,386 --> 01:07:38,387
Is the rice boiling?

1779
01:07:38,788 --> 01:07:40,256
IT NEEDS TO BOIL BY NOW…

1780
01:07:40,322 --> 01:07:42,725
-It really won't.
-If it won't, it's an emergency.

1781
01:07:42,792 --> 01:07:43,893
It really is.

1782
01:07:43,959 --> 01:07:46,228
It was up there for five minutes,
but it wouldn't boil.

1783
01:07:46,295 --> 01:07:49,031
It's way too… This is crazy.

1784
01:07:49,098 --> 01:07:51,867
-Just resort to instant rice!
-Just use instant rice.

1785
01:07:51,934 --> 01:07:54,370
Start microwaving it for now.

1786
01:07:54,437 --> 01:07:56,472
-Microwave it first.
-Do it just in case.

1787
01:07:56,539 --> 01:07:57,373
I don't want to.

1788
01:07:57,440 --> 01:07:59,341
Ring the bell late.

1789
01:07:59,408 --> 01:08:01,310
-Yes, I should do that.
-Nine minutes.

1790
01:08:01,377 --> 01:08:03,079
He's using lots of meat too.

1791
01:08:03,145 --> 01:08:04,980
-I love the portion.
-Yes.

1792
01:08:05,047 --> 01:08:06,816
But you won't get all of that.

1793
01:08:06,882 --> 01:08:09,151
-He'll eat some of it.
-Yes.

1794
01:08:09,218 --> 01:08:10,920
What if he gets only that tiny piece?

1795
01:08:10,986 --> 01:08:12,688
They aren't all yours.

1796
01:08:13,889 --> 01:08:16,892
-Before it's done cooking?
-Then he just boils it down for a while.

1797
01:08:16,959 --> 01:08:18,828
-<i>Jjol-galbi,</i> right?
-Yes.

1798
01:08:18,894 --> 01:08:20,463
That's how you make it?

1799
01:08:20,529 --> 01:08:23,632
Chef Eun-yeong rolls the crêpe dough.

1800
01:08:23,699 --> 01:08:25,334
She's taking a while too.

1801
01:08:26,469 --> 01:08:27,636
She's flattening the dough. Wait.

1802
01:08:27,703 --> 01:08:28,871
-Dumpling filling.
-Filing.

1803
01:08:28,938 --> 01:08:31,407
She's deconstructing the dumplings.

1804
01:08:31,474 --> 01:08:33,142
-Dumplings?
-She's removing the skin.

1805
01:08:33,209 --> 01:08:35,911
What? Are you going
to put that into the wheat crêpe?

1806
01:08:35,978 --> 01:08:37,246
That's just dumpling.

1807
01:08:37,813 --> 01:08:40,282
-No.
-It's just another dumpling.

1808
01:08:40,349 --> 01:08:42,118
-Reversed dumpling.
-What is this?

1809
01:08:42,184 --> 01:08:44,854
-Reversed dumpling.
-Just serve those instead.

1810
01:08:44,920 --> 01:08:46,555
It tastes different.

1811
01:08:46,956 --> 01:08:48,657
SESAME OIL

1812
01:08:48,724 --> 01:08:50,326
You're not making the stuffing yourself?

1813
01:08:50,392 --> 01:08:52,461
-The shape's different.
-The shape's different?

1814
01:08:52,528 --> 01:08:53,562
The shape's different.

1815
01:08:53,629 --> 01:08:55,030
She just changed the skin.

1816
01:08:55,097 --> 01:08:56,332
Just swapped the skin.

1817
01:08:57,533 --> 01:08:59,001
It's a bigger dumpling.

1818
01:08:59,068 --> 01:09:00,703
You're right.

1819
01:09:00,769 --> 01:09:03,806
-Like a scallion pancake.
-I get it.

1820
01:09:03,873 --> 01:09:06,408
-It looks good.
-It's like a pancake.

1821
01:09:06,475 --> 01:09:08,944
But the filling will taste the same.

1822
01:09:09,011 --> 01:09:11,547
Everyone, it'll taste different
when pan-fried.

1823
01:09:12,281 --> 01:09:13,115
COOKING WITH SPIRIT!

1824
01:09:13,182 --> 01:09:15,718
-Just pan-fry dumplings.
-She makes her case.

1825
01:09:15,784 --> 01:09:18,120
See what it's like when pan-fried.

1826
01:09:18,187 --> 01:09:21,924
-That rice is boiling.
-It's boiling.

1827
01:09:22,191 --> 01:09:23,325
WHOA

1828
01:09:23,392 --> 01:09:26,061
-Reduce the heat.
-No, it has to boil hard.

1829
01:09:26,128 --> 01:09:28,163
Why are you keeping the lid open?

1830
01:09:28,230 --> 01:09:31,600
If he closes it, the water will boil over,
and he won't have enough.

1831
01:09:31,667 --> 01:09:33,269
OPEN THE LID AND RAISE THE HEAT!

1832
01:09:33,335 --> 01:09:34,403
Gosh.

1833
01:09:34,470 --> 01:09:36,639
He's practically dining there.

1834
01:09:37,540 --> 01:09:40,042
That water will seep into the rice.

1835
01:09:40,109 --> 01:09:41,677
He closes the lid.

1836
01:09:41,744 --> 01:09:44,613
He closed the lid
with 7 minutes and 30 seconds left.

1837
01:09:44,680 --> 01:09:45,881
THE PROBLEM IS JJOL-GALBI

1838
01:09:45,948 --> 01:09:48,150
Does he have enough time
for that to boil down?

1839
01:09:48,217 --> 01:09:50,419
I don't think it'll boil down.

1840
01:09:50,486 --> 01:09:52,521
-I do feel a bit anxious.
-Don't you?

1841
01:09:52,588 --> 01:09:56,158
It may become <i>galbi-tang,</i> like soup,
and not <i>jjol-galbi.</i>

1842
01:09:56,225 --> 01:09:57,226
Garlic.

1843
01:09:57,760 --> 01:09:59,828
THE KEY IS BOILING IT DOWN ON TIME

1844
01:09:59,895 --> 01:10:00,729
Five minutes.

1845
01:10:00,796 --> 01:10:02,398
-Five minutes, Jung-hwan.
-Already?

1846
01:10:02,464 --> 01:10:03,966
It popped.

1847
01:10:04,033 --> 01:10:06,969
-Oh, my. It popped.
-Her dumpling popped!

1848
01:10:07,203 --> 01:10:08,771
MY DUMPLINGS!

1849
01:10:08,837 --> 01:10:12,508
-Goodness, it's popped.
-You can just use regular dumplings.

1850
01:10:12,575 --> 01:10:14,743
But the back is fine.

1851
01:10:14,810 --> 01:10:16,078
The back is fine, so she's okay.

1852
01:10:16,145 --> 01:10:19,248
These aren't dumplings,
so you're supposed to eat them popped.

1853
01:10:19,315 --> 01:10:20,249
They're not?

1854
01:10:20,316 --> 01:10:21,750
-They're not?
-She's extraordinary.

1855
01:10:21,817 --> 01:10:23,219
She is.

1856
01:10:23,285 --> 01:10:24,787
Are you pan-frying the dumplings?

1857
01:10:25,521 --> 01:10:27,957
Chef Eun-yeong is frying
the popped dumplings.

1858
01:10:28,023 --> 01:10:29,625
The flounder is getting braised too.

1859
01:10:29,692 --> 01:10:32,628
She needs much more time
to boil down the flounder.

1860
01:10:32,695 --> 01:10:35,431
And his <i>jjol-galbi</i> also hasn't
boiled down much.

1861
01:10:35,497 --> 01:10:36,932
This is more like soup <i>galbi.</i>

1862
01:10:36,999 --> 01:10:38,734
-Soup <i>galbi?</i>
-Right?

1863
01:10:38,801 --> 01:10:40,202
It's like <i>galbi-jjim</i>, braised short ribs.

1864
01:10:40,269 --> 01:10:42,104
Why don't you taste the broth
for each side?

1865
01:10:42,171 --> 01:10:44,506
-Let me try the broth.
-Yes, the flounder broth.

1866
01:10:44,573 --> 01:10:46,642
-The flounder first?
-Yes.

1867
01:10:46,709 --> 01:10:47,743
There's no time.

1868
01:10:47,810 --> 01:10:49,478
-Visit Nam-no first.
-Nam-no?

1869
01:10:49,545 --> 01:10:51,814
Nam-no's <i>jjol-galbi</i> first.

1870
01:10:51,880 --> 01:10:55,150
-Try the broth.
<i>-Jjol-galbi.</i>

1871
01:10:55,217 --> 01:10:59,788
Yes, does it taste good enough
to make Chef Eun-yeong scared?

1872
01:11:00,055 --> 01:11:01,090
EYES OPEN WIDE

1873
01:11:02,091 --> 01:11:04,126
I don't think it's quite boiled down yet.

1874
01:11:04,460 --> 01:11:05,294
OH, NO

1875
01:11:05,361 --> 01:11:06,562
Only three minutes left.

1876
01:11:06,629 --> 01:11:08,831
-Three minutes.
-Three minutes left.

1877
01:11:08,897 --> 01:11:11,867
This needs some depth in the flavor,
but it's on the surface level.

1878
01:11:11,934 --> 01:11:14,270
-I see.
-Like unfinished <i>galbi-jjim</i>.

1879
01:11:14,336 --> 01:11:16,438
-See?
-The sauce is too bland.

1880
01:11:16,505 --> 01:11:18,173
It'll be nice once it boils down.

1881
01:11:18,240 --> 01:11:19,742
He probably has a plan.

1882
01:11:19,808 --> 01:11:22,845
-The flounder lands on a stone plate.
-A stone plate?

1883
01:11:22,911 --> 01:11:24,747
It'll boil down over time, right?

1884
01:11:24,813 --> 01:11:27,116
Are you not using the flounder soup?

1885
01:11:27,182 --> 01:11:29,051
I am. I'll use it as sauce.

1886
01:11:29,118 --> 01:11:30,452
-As sauce.
-That's the sauce.

1887
01:11:30,519 --> 01:11:33,422
-The flounder.
-Yes, the flounder sauce.

1888
01:11:33,489 --> 01:11:38,227
Does it taste like FloundeCrazy or what?

1889
01:11:38,294 --> 01:11:39,128
FROWNS

1890
01:11:39,194 --> 01:11:41,764
-It's not crazy.
-It's not, right?

1891
01:11:41,830 --> 01:11:43,499
-What now?
-Hurry up.

1892
01:11:43,565 --> 01:11:45,501
-I have to braise it.
-Do it quickly.

1893
01:11:45,567 --> 01:11:47,603
-It's too bland.
-It's bland?

1894
01:11:47,670 --> 01:11:48,504
I have to braise it.

1895
01:11:48,570 --> 01:11:50,439
And it tastes a bit fishy.

1896
01:11:50,506 --> 01:11:51,407
It's fishy?

1897
01:11:52,441 --> 01:11:53,842
GOSH…

1898
01:11:53,909 --> 01:11:55,044
Use vinegar.

1899
01:11:55,110 --> 01:11:58,047
He had said
the flounder wasn't in great condition.

1900
01:11:58,113 --> 01:12:00,683
-It's a bit fishy, right?
-It is.

1901
01:12:00,749 --> 01:12:02,951
-You have to get rid of the smell.
-You really do.

1902
01:12:03,018 --> 01:12:04,553
And this one needs to boil down.

1903
01:12:04,620 --> 01:12:06,355
-One minute.
-Hurry up.

1904
01:12:06,422 --> 01:12:08,290
Because the fish wasn't fresh,

1905
01:12:08,357 --> 01:12:11,527
it could get fishy
as it lets the flavor out into the water.

1906
01:12:11,593 --> 01:12:13,829
-It's a bit fishy.
-Nam-no is at an advantage.

1907
01:12:13,896 --> 01:12:15,698
-One minute left.
-Hurry up.

1908
01:12:15,764 --> 01:12:17,099
You have to put it together.

1909
01:12:17,166 --> 01:12:19,735
7. SIFT POLLACK BROTH
AND POUR IN A THERMOS

1910
01:12:20,269 --> 01:12:22,237
You have 30 seconds left!

1911
01:12:22,304 --> 01:12:24,340
You have to plate your dishes.

1912
01:12:24,406 --> 01:12:27,109
-That looks insane.
-Start plating the food.

1913
01:12:27,176 --> 01:12:29,311
Whoever ends later will have an advantage.

1914
01:12:29,378 --> 01:12:31,780
They'll let each other go first.

1915
01:12:31,847 --> 01:12:34,049
Then no one will ring the bell.

1916
01:12:34,116 --> 01:12:36,552
-They're both staying behind.
-What if no one rings it?

1917
01:12:36,618 --> 01:12:38,654
That's why they aren't doing it?

1918
01:12:39,254 --> 01:12:40,389
It's a mind game.

1919
01:12:40,456 --> 01:12:42,658
Ring it and finish, Nam-no.

1920
01:12:42,725 --> 01:12:44,460
SHOULD I RING IT?

1921
01:12:45,160 --> 01:12:46,462
She's suddenly nice.

1922
01:12:46,528 --> 01:12:49,298
-Who'll ring the bell first? Ring it.
-You can go first.

1923
01:12:49,365 --> 01:12:51,433
He has to let the rice sit
and boil down the dish too.

1924
01:12:53,669 --> 01:12:55,137
Time's up,

1925
01:12:55,938 --> 01:12:57,272
but no one's ringing the bell.

1926
01:12:57,339 --> 01:12:58,774
Just do rock-paper-scissors.

1927
01:12:58,841 --> 01:13:00,976
SLOWLY POURING THE SAUCE

1928
01:13:03,612 --> 01:13:06,281
TOPPING WITH GREEN ONION

1929
01:13:06,348 --> 01:13:09,385
-And now. Five. Four. Three.
-Five. Four. Three.

1930
01:13:09,451 --> 01:13:11,687
USING HIS TIME DOWN TO THE LAST SECOND!

1931
01:13:11,754 --> 01:13:13,088
-Time's up.
-Ring the bell.

1932
01:13:13,155 --> 01:13:13,989
I'M AT MY LIMIT

1933
01:13:14,323 --> 01:13:16,024
IN THE END, NAM-NO FINISHES FIRST

1934
01:13:16,091 --> 01:13:16,959
-Nam-no.
-He's older.

1935
01:13:17,025 --> 01:13:19,228
Eun-yeong waits, and then rings the bell.

1936
01:13:19,294 --> 01:13:21,697
THAT WAS FIERCE

1937
01:13:21,764 --> 01:13:23,699
Eun-yeong serves her dish too.

1938
01:13:23,766 --> 01:13:25,067
They're done.

1939
01:13:25,134 --> 01:13:26,268
That was amazing.

1940
01:13:26,335 --> 01:13:28,804
You two were stalling too much.

1941
01:13:28,871 --> 01:13:32,307
Both chefs made
all the dishes for Seon-min.

1942
01:13:32,374 --> 01:13:34,643
-Gosh.
-The air is different from the first one.

1943
01:13:34,710 --> 01:13:37,246
-Yes.
-Any takes on these hearty dishes?

1944
01:13:37,312 --> 01:13:40,582
I love them, but I can't even dare
to imagine what they'd be like.

1945
01:13:41,216 --> 01:13:43,419
-I'll have to try them to find out.
-Of course.

1946
01:13:43,485 --> 01:13:45,487
First, Chef Nam-no's dish,

1947
01:13:45,554 --> 01:13:47,823
Did You Have to Scrape the Bowl Clean?

1948
01:13:47,890 --> 01:13:50,492
-The Bowl Clean?
-The Bowl Clean?

1949
01:13:50,826 --> 01:13:54,029
JJOL-GALBI THAT MAKES YOU
WANT TO SCRAPE THE SAUCE OFF

1950
01:13:55,097 --> 01:13:57,566
TANGY AND SPICY RADISH SALAD WITH KELP

1951
01:13:58,867 --> 01:14:01,970
CHEF YOON NAM-NO'S
DID YOU HAVE TO SCRAPE THE BOWL CLEAN?

1952
01:14:02,037 --> 01:14:04,540
May I give you a hand? And cut your meat?

1953
01:14:04,606 --> 01:14:06,275
You're coming over?

1954
01:14:06,341 --> 01:14:08,043
-What will he do?
-One second.

1955
01:14:08,110 --> 01:14:09,478
What's with the bowl? Are you eating too?

1956
01:14:09,778 --> 01:14:11,447
REASONABLE SUSPICION

1957
01:14:11,513 --> 01:14:13,715
-I wouldn't.
-Do you want us to give you a seat?

1958
01:14:13,782 --> 01:14:15,117
I thought you wanted to eat with him.

1959
01:14:15,684 --> 01:14:17,319
After all, you brought two bowls.

1960
01:14:17,820 --> 01:14:19,621
PREPARES THE JJOL-GALBI HIMSELF

1961
01:14:20,289 --> 01:14:21,323
Whoa.

1962
01:14:21,390 --> 01:14:22,825
-Wow.
-That looks good.

1963
01:14:22,891 --> 01:14:23,959
Shingled hedgehog.

1964
01:14:24,026 --> 01:14:25,227
Green onion.

1965
01:14:25,294 --> 01:14:26,895
-Kelp.
-Kelp.

1966
01:14:26,962 --> 01:14:28,464
Spicy radish salad.

1967
01:14:28,530 --> 01:14:31,133
Please try everything at once.

1968
01:14:31,200 --> 01:14:32,734
You might as well chew it for him.

1969
01:14:32,801 --> 01:14:34,369
He's just suggesting.

1970
01:14:34,436 --> 01:14:35,771
The hot pot rice. This is it.

1971
01:14:35,838 --> 01:14:37,506
-The hot pot rice.
-Is it ready?

1972
01:14:37,573 --> 01:14:40,375
-Can you open it?
-It's still hot.

1973
01:14:40,576 --> 01:14:41,810
CURIOUS

1974
01:14:42,211 --> 01:14:44,780
TA-DA

1975
01:14:45,180 --> 01:14:46,715
IT LOOKS INSANE

1976
01:14:46,782 --> 01:14:48,917
He did this in 15 minutes?

1977
01:14:48,984 --> 01:14:51,687
That's done.
Do you see the scorched rice underneath?

1978
01:14:51,753 --> 01:14:53,655
Are you pouring something
from the tumbler?

1979
01:14:53,722 --> 01:14:54,990
-Sounds good.
-I'm making <i>sungnyung.</i>

1980
01:14:55,057 --> 01:14:56,859
You called it your secret sauce.

1981
01:14:56,925 --> 01:14:59,328
<i>-Hwangtae-guk,</i> dried pollack soup.
-I see.

1982
01:15:00,062 --> 01:15:00,896
Dried pollack soup.

1983
01:15:00,963 --> 01:15:02,931
-That was why you made that.
-It looks good.

1984
01:15:02,998 --> 01:15:04,399
I think that'd be the best one.

1985
01:15:04,466 --> 01:15:05,901
-Yes, that one.
-It's not plain water.

1986
01:15:05,968 --> 01:15:08,570
-Cover the lid.
-It's already delicious.

1987
01:15:08,637 --> 01:15:10,372
The order was titled Dishwasher Energy.

1988
01:15:10,439 --> 01:15:13,008
You'll have to finish everything to pass.

1989
01:15:13,075 --> 01:15:14,977
This is a lot more than I thought.

1990
01:15:15,043 --> 01:15:16,912
Even everything in the tumbler.

1991
01:15:17,179 --> 01:15:18,113
THIS?

1992
01:15:18,747 --> 01:15:20,516
It'll end up burning the top of my mouth.

1993
01:15:20,582 --> 01:15:23,352
-Dishwasher Energy was the order.
-He'll have to call the repair service.

1994
01:15:23,418 --> 01:15:25,988
-You don't have to finish everything.
-<i>Jjol-galbi</i> and spicy radish salad.

1995
01:15:27,055 --> 01:15:29,458
THE DISHWASHER TURNS ON

1996
01:15:29,525 --> 01:15:32,394
Was the meat seasoned well?

1997
01:15:33,128 --> 01:15:33,962
I hope it's tender.

1998
01:15:34,029 --> 01:15:35,931
-It must be good.
-It shouldn't be tough.

1999
01:15:35,998 --> 01:15:38,400
And the radish salad has to be good too.

2000
01:15:38,467 --> 01:15:40,202
That's a nice smile.

2001
01:15:40,269 --> 01:15:41,537
HEY! = IT'S DELICIOUS!

2002
01:15:41,770 --> 01:15:42,604
GOSH

2003
01:15:43,071 --> 01:15:44,940
He was a national guest before,
but he's now a thug.

2004
01:15:45,007 --> 01:15:46,542
Before his success. It's the prequel.

2005
01:15:46,808 --> 01:15:49,011
MY GOOD-FOR-NOTHING MISTER

2006
01:15:49,077 --> 01:15:49,978
Boss.

2007
01:15:51,413 --> 01:15:52,381
Man.

2008
01:15:52,447 --> 01:15:54,583
What is it? Why are you surprised?

2009
01:15:54,650 --> 01:15:55,717
It's the taste of my kind.

2010
01:15:55,784 --> 01:15:57,486
-The taste of his kind?
-"His kind."

2011
01:15:57,553 --> 01:15:59,488
-He gets you?
-Yes, he gets me.

2012
01:16:00,188 --> 01:16:01,757
-Is he one of you?
-He is.

2013
01:16:02,257 --> 01:16:04,226
-Next, the hot pot rice.
-That looks good.

2014
01:16:04,293 --> 01:16:05,460
What did you put in here?

2015
01:16:05,527 --> 01:16:08,564
<i>Shingled hedgehog, pickled victory onion,</i>

2016
01:16:08,630 --> 01:16:10,232
<i>and shingled hedgehog water.</i>

2017
01:16:10,299 --> 01:16:12,334
-Look at the color.
-It's impossible to taste bad.

2018
01:16:12,401 --> 01:16:14,603
That looks so good. He eats so well.

2019
01:16:14,670 --> 01:16:16,138
GASP(?)

2020
01:16:16,772 --> 01:16:18,407
Slow down.

2021
01:16:18,473 --> 01:16:19,508
It's hot.

2022
01:16:19,575 --> 01:16:20,842
-But that's how you eat it.
-Yes.

2023
01:16:20,909 --> 01:16:23,712
You have to sweat while you eat it.

2024
01:16:23,779 --> 01:16:25,380
WHAT'S THE QUICK HOT POT RICE LIKE?

2025
01:16:25,948 --> 01:16:27,082
-Hmm?
-What?

2026
01:16:27,649 --> 01:16:29,117
-Is it cooked?
-I guess not.

2027
01:16:29,184 --> 01:16:30,886
-Was it cooked?
-Are rice and beans cooked?

2028
01:16:30,953 --> 01:16:32,454
-Right. The beans.
-Are lentils cooked?

2029
01:16:33,121 --> 01:16:34,056
It's cooked nicely.

2030
01:16:34,122 --> 01:16:35,624
EVERYTHING IS COOKED WELL

2031
01:16:35,691 --> 01:16:37,225
-Did you use <i>doenjang?</i>
-What is it?

2032
01:16:37,292 --> 01:16:38,760
-What is it?
-Does it smell savory?

2033
01:16:38,827 --> 01:16:40,696
-Yes, pleasant and savory.
-Shingled hedgehog.

2034
01:16:40,762 --> 01:16:42,197
It's the shingled hedgehog water.

2035
01:16:42,264 --> 01:16:44,199
Because of its water?

2036
01:16:44,266 --> 01:16:45,500
It's really delicious

2037
01:16:45,567 --> 01:16:47,603
if you make rice
with shingled hedgehog water.

2038
01:16:47,669 --> 01:16:49,338
-Is that the savory smell?
-Yes.

2039
01:16:49,404 --> 01:16:52,307
It almost reminds me of <i>doenjang-jjigae.</i>

2040
01:16:52,374 --> 01:16:56,044
Seon-min knows a thing or two
about <i>doenjang.</i>

2041
01:16:56,111 --> 01:16:57,245
A BIG BITE

2042
01:16:57,512 --> 01:16:58,747
GASP

2043
01:16:58,814 --> 01:17:00,916
You should grab some radish salad.

2044
01:17:00,983 --> 01:17:03,385
-It's too hot.
-Go for it now.

2045
01:17:03,919 --> 01:17:05,520
INTO MY MOUTH, NOW

2046
01:17:05,587 --> 01:17:07,356
Kelp with radish?

2047
01:17:07,422 --> 01:17:08,323
What does that do?

2048
01:17:08,390 --> 01:17:10,726
-The radish must be good.
-It must pair nicely.

2049
01:17:10,792 --> 01:17:11,627
It's the texture.

2050
01:17:12,527 --> 01:17:13,662
For pickled radish,

2051
01:17:13,729 --> 01:17:16,765
there are the regular ones
and the thinner ones, right?

2052
01:17:16,832 --> 01:17:18,133
I prefer the thin ones.

2053
01:17:18,500 --> 01:17:19,635
ALL RIGHT

2054
01:17:20,702 --> 01:17:22,004
He got lucky.

2055
01:17:22,070 --> 01:17:24,206
<i>And I can't complain about the kelp.</i>

2056
01:17:24,272 --> 01:17:26,174
I love its texture.

2057
01:17:26,241 --> 01:17:27,643
-It adds a bite.
-Yes.

2058
01:17:27,709 --> 01:17:29,911
You'll see
vegetable wraps in front of you.

2059
01:17:29,978 --> 01:17:32,114
Pick up a piece of lettuce
and perilla leaf,

2060
01:17:32,180 --> 01:17:34,182
then make a wrap with everything.

2061
01:17:34,816 --> 01:17:38,420
-That looks good.
-We can tell that he's made lots of wraps.

2062
01:17:38,487 --> 01:17:41,556
He looks much more comfortable.
It's different from the last round.

2063
01:17:41,623 --> 01:17:43,258
<i>-Yes.</i>
<i>-He seems more natural.</i>

2064
01:17:43,325 --> 01:17:45,360
<i>He seemed really awkward earlier.</i>

2065
01:17:46,395 --> 01:17:47,996
A spoon and chopsticks

2066
01:17:48,063 --> 01:17:50,132
suit him better than a fork and a knife.

2067
01:17:51,166 --> 01:17:52,234
He's enjoying it so much.

2068
01:17:53,468 --> 01:17:55,103
-Gosh.
-"Gosh."

2069
01:17:55,170 --> 01:17:56,638
He's such son-in-law material.

2070
01:17:57,005 --> 01:17:58,407
IT'S TORTURE TO JUST WATCH

2071
01:17:58,974 --> 01:17:59,908
You're good.

2072
01:17:59,975 --> 01:18:01,309
TEEHEE

2073
01:18:01,376 --> 01:18:02,744
He's good,

2074
01:18:02,811 --> 01:18:04,646
and he knows me well.

2075
01:18:04,713 --> 01:18:06,782
His strategy worked too well.

2076
01:18:06,848 --> 01:18:08,750
I feel like he saw right through me.

2077
01:18:09,051 --> 01:18:10,018
SNIPED

2078
01:18:10,085 --> 01:18:11,219
"How does he know me so well?"

2079
01:18:11,286 --> 01:18:13,522
-Nice try.
-Nice try, Eun-yeong.

2080
01:18:13,588 --> 01:18:14,890
Nothing to criticize about it?

2081
01:18:14,956 --> 01:18:16,958
-Criticize?
-Is the meat too tough?

2082
01:18:19,661 --> 01:18:20,529
This one isn't cut.

2083
01:18:22,197 --> 01:18:23,765
I left it whole on purpose.

2084
01:18:23,832 --> 01:18:25,133
-On purpose?
-Yes.

2085
01:18:25,200 --> 01:18:27,436
You can slurp it in like noodles.

2086
01:18:27,502 --> 01:18:28,737
What are you talking about?

2087
01:18:28,804 --> 01:18:30,405
I know I eat a lot, but…

2088
01:18:31,239 --> 01:18:32,507
I can't eat this in one bite.

2089
01:18:32,574 --> 01:18:34,609
That's my entire breakfast.

2090
01:18:34,676 --> 01:18:36,778
He can eat that over two days.

2091
01:18:36,845 --> 01:18:39,915
I got the shingled hedgehog as a present,

2092
01:18:39,981 --> 01:18:43,652
and the only way I knew how to eat it
was <i>samgyetang,</i> chicken soup.

2093
01:18:44,286 --> 01:18:47,322
I love that I can eat it like this.

2094
01:18:48,056 --> 01:18:48,924
THE DISHWASHER IS ON

2095
01:18:48,990 --> 01:18:50,125
He eats so well.

2096
01:18:50,492 --> 01:18:51,827
ONE BITE AFTER ANOTHER

2097
01:18:51,893 --> 01:18:53,195
He makes it look so good.

2098
01:18:53,261 --> 01:18:54,629
He makes my mouth water.

2099
01:18:54,896 --> 01:18:55,864
SPICY RADISH SALAD

2100
01:18:57,499 --> 01:18:58,333
SUCK IT IN♥

2101
01:18:58,400 --> 01:19:00,602
-He destroyed it.
-You don't have to eat that much.

2102
01:19:00,669 --> 01:19:02,170
THE STRONGEST CLEANING POWER(?)

2103
01:19:02,404 --> 01:19:03,739
FEELING PROUD

2104
01:19:04,306 --> 01:19:05,674
TEMPERATURE CHECK

2105
01:19:05,741 --> 01:19:07,476
Lastly, go for the climax.

2106
01:19:07,542 --> 01:19:09,311
Once he has a bite, it'll be game over.

2107
01:19:09,578 --> 01:19:10,679
PULLING DOWN

2108
01:19:11,847 --> 01:19:13,448
THE HUMAN DISHWASHER IS STILL HUMAN

2109
01:19:14,182 --> 01:19:15,383
-Wow.
-Wow.

2110
01:19:15,450 --> 01:19:16,685
It's steamy.

2111
01:19:16,752 --> 01:19:17,686
Look at that.

2112
01:19:17,753 --> 01:19:21,156
I've never seen that kind of <i>nurungji</i>.
It's hard to imagine.

2113
01:19:22,424 --> 01:19:24,593
First of all, it smells so good.

2114
01:19:25,160 --> 01:19:26,828
Pollack and shingled hedgehog.

2115
01:19:26,895 --> 01:19:27,963
It's such a solid meal.

2116
01:19:28,029 --> 01:19:29,664
That'll be the best bite.

2117
01:19:29,731 --> 01:19:30,732
SLURP

2118
01:19:31,867 --> 01:19:33,001
Gosh.

2119
01:19:33,068 --> 01:19:34,669
What's it like?

2120
01:19:35,203 --> 01:19:36,037
CHUCKLES

2121
01:19:37,405 --> 01:19:38,406
YOU♥

2122
01:19:38,473 --> 01:19:39,674
-What is it?
-Boss.

2123
01:19:39,741 --> 01:19:41,676
-Very interesting.
-It is?

2124
01:19:41,743 --> 01:19:42,644
DRINKS EVERY DROP

2125
01:19:43,812 --> 01:19:45,313
CURIOUS

2126
01:19:45,380 --> 01:19:47,783
It's like carefully mixing

2127
01:19:47,849 --> 01:19:51,186
elaborately boiled broth
with perfectly cooked rice

2128
01:19:51,253 --> 01:19:52,788
that was prepared with lots of care.

2129
01:19:53,588 --> 01:19:55,757
I've never had anything like this before.

2130
01:19:55,824 --> 01:19:58,527
It's like something I could eat
at a classy Korean full-course restaurant.

2131
01:19:59,094 --> 01:20:03,832
I've seen places that serve
green tea to pour into the hot pot.

2132
01:20:03,899 --> 01:20:05,367
That's how some people eat it.

2133
01:20:06,334 --> 01:20:08,370
THE SECOND BITE IS STILL AS GOOD

2134
01:20:08,436 --> 01:20:11,306
Does it even feel like
you're getting entertained?

2135
01:20:11,373 --> 01:20:13,308
The thing I love about this most

2136
01:20:13,375 --> 01:20:16,178
is that this is something
I can have with my parents too.

2137
01:20:16,244 --> 01:20:19,014
It's something that
our parents would love.

2138
01:20:19,481 --> 01:20:22,083
EVEN THE NURUNGJI WAS A SUCCESS

2139
01:20:22,150 --> 01:20:24,452
Excuse me. The fish is getting cold.

2140
01:20:24,519 --> 01:20:27,689
All right. Next up, Chef Eun-yeong's dish.

2141
01:20:27,756 --> 01:20:29,024
FloundeCrazy.

2142
01:20:29,391 --> 01:20:32,294
CANTONESE-STYLE BRAISED FLOUNDER
WITH LOTS OF SIRAEGI

2143
01:20:33,428 --> 01:20:34,596
AND SPECIAL WHEAT CRÊPE

2144
01:20:34,663 --> 01:20:36,298
(NOT A DUMPLING)

2145
01:20:37,165 --> 01:20:40,735
CHEF PARK EUN-YEONG'S FLOUNDECRAZY

2146
01:20:41,102 --> 01:20:42,504
BRAISED FLOUNDER IS SERVED

2147
01:20:43,338 --> 01:20:44,606
The dumpling.

2148
01:20:45,373 --> 01:20:46,675
Wheat crêpe, not a dumpling.

2149
01:20:46,741 --> 01:20:48,243
It's wheat crêpe, not a dumpling.

2150
01:20:48,310 --> 01:20:49,744
It's a wheat crêpe, right?

2151
01:20:49,811 --> 01:20:51,847
But you didn't serve it
with any sauce, right?

2152
01:20:51,913 --> 01:20:54,783
There's no sauce.
First, have some fish meat,

2153
01:20:54,850 --> 01:20:56,551
then rip the crêpe

2154
01:20:56,618 --> 01:20:59,087
and have it with the sauce and <i>siraegi.</i>

2155
01:20:59,154 --> 01:21:00,856
-Okay.
<i>-Siraegi</i> sounds good.

2156
01:21:00,922 --> 01:21:05,327
I wonder what the flounder is like.
She used it to make broth too.

2157
01:21:05,393 --> 01:21:07,963
Jung-hwan had mentioned
that it could taste a bit fishy.

2158
01:21:08,029 --> 01:21:09,297
Do you mind the fishiness?

2159
01:21:09,364 --> 01:21:12,167
An adequate amount of fishiness
may even be a plus.

2160
01:21:13,068 --> 01:21:13,935
I'll try it.

2161
01:21:14,002 --> 01:21:17,138
Flounder and <i>siraegi.</i>

2162
01:21:17,205 --> 01:21:18,340
Will they go well together?

2163
01:21:19,007 --> 01:21:20,675
YUM

2164
01:21:22,177 --> 01:21:23,678
She added chili oil too.

2165
01:21:23,745 --> 01:21:25,280
He chews really fast.

2166
01:21:25,881 --> 01:21:27,048
-He's really fast.
-I'm scared.

2167
01:21:27,115 --> 01:21:28,483
He moves his mouth very fast.

2168
01:21:28,550 --> 01:21:30,552
It's like watching at double speed.

2169
01:21:30,619 --> 01:21:33,488
Someone like Ji Sang-ryeol would say,
"He can dribble with his tongue."

2170
01:21:34,990 --> 01:21:36,391
THE BETTER IT IS, THE FASTER HE GETS

2171
01:21:36,625 --> 01:21:38,126
GOOD DRIBBLING ENERGY

2172
01:21:38,193 --> 01:21:40,362
THIS TIME, WITH THE CRÊPE

2173
01:21:40,428 --> 01:21:42,464
Top it with <i>siraegi.</i> Wait, that big?

2174
01:21:42,530 --> 01:21:43,732
Whoa. Just like that.

2175
01:21:44,933 --> 01:21:45,934
It looks good.

2176
01:21:46,635 --> 01:21:48,536
STRAIGHT INTO HIS MOUTH

2177
01:21:48,603 --> 01:21:50,472
He truly was the king of school lunch.

2178
01:21:50,839 --> 01:21:53,909
THE SCHOOL LUNCH KING ENERGY

2179
01:21:53,975 --> 01:21:55,911
-He's back again.
-He ate like this back then too.

2180
01:21:55,977 --> 01:21:58,246
-He was one of the top four.
-My mouth is watering.

2181
01:21:58,313 --> 01:22:00,448
-I'm hungry.
-Tell us about it.

2182
01:22:00,515 --> 01:22:04,819
Soaking and boiling <i>siraegi</i>
isn't an easy process.

2183
01:22:04,886 --> 01:22:06,655
-It's hard to make it tender.
-Right.

2184
01:22:06,721 --> 01:22:10,659
The fact that she did this
in 15 minutes is so impressive.

2185
01:22:10,725 --> 01:22:12,527
THANK YOU

2186
01:22:13,094 --> 01:22:14,296
It makes me crave a drink.

2187
01:22:14,362 --> 01:22:17,565
I'd want to come home
and drink soju over this.

2188
01:22:17,632 --> 01:22:19,000
WOULD GO WELL WITH SOME SOJU

2189
01:22:19,067 --> 01:22:21,403
And because I like spicy food,

2190
01:22:21,469 --> 01:22:24,005
it would've been nice if it were spicier.

2191
01:22:24,072 --> 01:22:24,906
Spicier than this?

2192
01:22:24,973 --> 01:22:27,475
There's a pleasant spice to it, though.

2193
01:22:27,542 --> 01:22:29,277
-Is the flounder fishy?
-I'd never say

2194
01:22:29,344 --> 01:22:31,980
something that smells like this is fishy.

2195
01:22:32,280 --> 01:22:34,182
BONUS POINTS
FOR JUST THE RIGHT AMOUNT OF PUNGENCE

2196
01:22:34,249 --> 01:22:36,084
He eats so well.

2197
01:22:36,151 --> 01:22:37,986
It's as if he's a driver at a pit stop.

2198
01:22:39,154 --> 01:22:40,121
RIGHT, THE RADISH SALAD

2199
01:22:40,188 --> 01:22:42,324
He goes for the radish.

2200
01:22:42,390 --> 01:22:44,859
-He makes it look so good.
-Look at his mouth move.

2201
01:22:45,160 --> 01:22:47,195
THE HUMAN DISHWASHER DOES NOT STOP

2202
01:22:48,063 --> 01:22:49,798
It's like these are
from the same restaurant.

2203
01:22:50,131 --> 01:22:52,567
THEY'LL DO SO WELL IF THEY PARTNER UP

2204
01:22:52,634 --> 01:22:54,936
-They go well together.
-The same restaurant?

2205
01:22:55,003 --> 01:22:57,539
Do you have some for COKUN?

2206
01:22:57,605 --> 01:23:00,809
-Just a bit. He doesn't eat much.
-He doesn't eat a lot.

2207
01:23:00,875 --> 01:23:02,510
We gave you only a little bit.

2208
01:23:02,577 --> 01:23:04,145
I love this portion.

2209
01:23:05,113 --> 01:23:07,415
It smells like it has to be good.

2210
01:23:07,816 --> 01:23:08,750
TOPPED WITH RADISH

2211
01:23:08,817 --> 01:23:10,418
Topped with some radish.

2212
01:23:11,886 --> 01:23:13,388
I want to make it look tasty too.

2213
01:23:13,655 --> 01:23:15,757
THE DISHWASHER MUKBANG WAS TOO INTENSE

2214
01:23:15,824 --> 01:23:17,359
Try chewing faster.

2215
01:23:17,425 --> 01:23:18,626
I'll give it a try.

2216
01:23:18,927 --> 01:23:20,462
HOW FAST WILL THE LIGHT EATER CHEW?

2217
01:23:20,528 --> 01:23:21,830
He wants to eat like him.

2218
01:23:24,699 --> 01:23:26,267
THIS IS HIS BEST

2219
01:23:26,334 --> 01:23:27,369
It looks delicious.

2220
01:23:27,669 --> 01:23:28,770
A DISHWASHER AT HEART

2221
01:23:28,837 --> 01:23:31,072
EXTREME SPEED DIFFERENCE

2222
01:23:31,139 --> 01:23:33,241
He's probably
never eaten like that before.

2223
01:23:33,308 --> 01:23:35,877
When you chew quickly like this,

2224
01:23:35,944 --> 01:23:38,813
it's hard to savor the flavor.

2225
01:23:38,880 --> 01:23:40,782
No, it amplifies the taste.

2226
01:23:41,116 --> 01:23:42,751
HE'S ON ANOTHER LEVEL…

2227
01:23:42,817 --> 01:23:46,154
I think I get
why he added kelp to the radish.

2228
01:23:46,221 --> 01:23:50,291
I love the texture that it adds.

2229
01:23:50,358 --> 01:23:52,327
-The crunchy and chewy bite.
-I love it.

2230
01:23:52,627 --> 01:23:54,729
A BITE OF HOT POT RICE

2231
01:23:55,430 --> 01:23:56,664
Whoa.

2232
01:23:56,731 --> 01:23:57,665
Excuse me.

2233
01:23:57,732 --> 01:24:00,168
Would you like to try this one?

2234
01:24:00,235 --> 01:24:02,137
He's like a restaurant owner.

2235
01:24:02,203 --> 01:24:04,305
Sir, you have to try this one.

2236
01:24:04,372 --> 01:24:06,041
He's a guy you can't say no to.

2237
01:24:06,107 --> 01:24:09,711
-Our hot pot rice is great.
-What makes it smell so delicious?

2238
01:24:09,778 --> 01:24:12,580
-Shingled hedgehog.
-Probably the mushroom.

2239
01:24:12,647 --> 01:24:14,182
NURUNGJI IS HERE

2240
01:24:14,249 --> 01:24:16,017
You're regretting giving me this.

2241
01:24:16,084 --> 01:24:17,018
Yes.

2242
01:24:17,085 --> 01:24:18,820
Yes, but you're my customer.

2243
01:24:19,821 --> 01:24:21,089
"Customer"?

2244
01:24:22,424 --> 01:24:24,526
THE OWNER FEELS PROUD

2245
01:24:25,293 --> 01:24:27,328
The shingled hedgehog plays
such an important role.

2246
01:24:27,395 --> 01:24:29,497
-Some say it's better than pine mushroom.
-I get it.

2247
01:24:29,564 --> 01:24:31,066
-Unlike I expected…
-Does it smell good?

2248
01:24:31,132 --> 01:24:32,934
-No, it's sweet.
-Sweet?

2249
01:24:33,001 --> 01:24:34,536
How can I put this?

2250
01:24:34,602 --> 01:24:37,672
It makes the rice taste sweet too,
but in a positive way.

2251
01:24:37,739 --> 01:24:38,807
Uh-huh.

2252
01:24:38,873 --> 01:24:41,142
-That's why he put it in.
-This is delicious.

2253
01:24:41,209 --> 01:24:42,510
Whoa, another bite?

2254
01:24:44,145 --> 01:24:45,280
I've eaten enough

2255
01:24:45,346 --> 01:24:47,215
to last until 6:00 p.m. tomorrow.

2256
01:24:47,282 --> 01:24:48,983
HIS BIGGEST BINGE EVER(?)

2257
01:24:49,050 --> 01:24:51,586
Next, Chef Eun-yeong's dish.

2258
01:24:51,653 --> 01:24:53,321
Is the dumpling under the fish?

2259
01:24:53,388 --> 01:24:54,989
-It's a crêpe.
-Crêpe.

2260
01:24:55,056 --> 01:24:56,658
-Yes, crêpe.
-It's a crêpe.

2261
01:24:56,724 --> 01:24:58,760
-It's not a dumpling.
-It's a crêpe.

2262
01:24:58,827 --> 01:25:02,197
You removed the dumpling skin
to make that.

2263
01:25:02,263 --> 01:25:04,265
-That's a good technique.
-The flounder.

2264
01:25:04,332 --> 01:25:06,000
ONE BIG BITE

2265
01:25:06,568 --> 01:25:08,803
Flounder topped with <i>siraegi.</i>

2266
01:25:08,870 --> 01:25:11,639
I also don't think this is fishy.

2267
01:25:11,706 --> 01:25:12,841
It's like dried yellow corvina.

2268
01:25:14,008 --> 01:25:15,577
That's nice, then.

2269
01:25:15,643 --> 01:25:17,846
But the other piece
is definitely a dumpling.

2270
01:25:19,414 --> 01:25:21,015
So obviously a dumpling.

2271
01:25:22,350 --> 01:25:24,385
I guess you've never had crêpe before.

2272
01:25:24,452 --> 01:25:27,122
-It's a delicious dumpling.
-A delicious dumpling.

2273
01:25:27,188 --> 01:25:28,456
Those dumplings are good.

2274
01:25:28,523 --> 01:25:30,358
-They are.
-Those are good ones.

2275
01:25:30,425 --> 01:25:34,362
It's now time to choose.

2276
01:25:34,429 --> 01:25:36,965
<i>Press one to choose Chef Eun-yeong,</i>

2277
01:25:37,031 --> 01:25:39,634
<i>and two to choose Chef Nam-no.</i>

2278
01:25:39,701 --> 01:25:42,570
Make your decision and press the button!

2279
01:25:44,405 --> 01:25:45,273
-Did you press it?
-Yes.

2280
01:25:45,340 --> 01:25:48,009
Which one of them did he choose?

2281
01:25:48,076 --> 01:25:49,811
Let's find out the result.

2282
01:25:49,878 --> 01:25:50,879
Show us!

2283
01:25:51,679 --> 01:25:52,914
Dishwasher Energy.

2284
01:25:52,981 --> 01:25:54,782
Both chefs have been on a winning streak.

2285
01:25:54,849 --> 01:25:57,218
And they've both won twice
and lost twice this year.

2286
01:25:57,285 --> 01:25:59,787
Will Nam-no take
his fourth consecutive victory?

2287
01:25:59,854 --> 01:26:02,323
Or will this be Eun-yeong's
third win in a row?

2288
01:26:02,390 --> 01:26:05,093
Who did Lee Seon-min choose?

2289
01:26:05,593 --> 01:26:07,328
Who's the winner?

2290
01:26:07,395 --> 01:26:08,496
Who won?

2291
01:26:09,631 --> 01:26:11,099
YOON NAM-NO WINS

2292
01:26:11,332 --> 01:26:12,867
DID YOU HAVE
TO SCRAPE THE BOWL CLEAN? WINS

2293
01:26:12,934 --> 01:26:14,102
Nam-no wins.

2294
01:26:14,669 --> 01:26:16,704
Yoon Nam-no won, just as we expected.

2295
01:26:16,771 --> 01:26:19,607
-Great job.
-Nam-no had an advantage for this one.

2296
01:26:19,674 --> 01:26:21,376
YOON NAM-NO'S 4TH STRAIGHT WIN

2297
01:26:21,442 --> 01:26:23,444
-The same kind.
-My kind.

2298
01:26:23,511 --> 01:26:26,080
If I had become a chef,

2299
01:26:26,147 --> 01:26:27,448
I would've made this.

2300
01:26:27,749 --> 01:26:30,485
PREFERENCES MATCH BY 100%

2301
01:26:30,552 --> 01:26:32,720
<i>Jjol-galbi</i> was excellent and delicious,

2302
01:26:32,787 --> 01:26:36,324
<i>but this </i>sungnyung<i> made</i>
<i>with scorched rice and pollack broth</i>

2303
01:26:36,391 --> 01:26:41,296
was something I'd get from the classiest
Korean full-course meal restaurants.

2304
01:26:41,362 --> 01:26:43,731
Chef Eun-yeong's dish
was something that I really love.

2305
01:26:43,798 --> 01:26:46,501
And you made it very nicely for me,

2306
01:26:46,568 --> 01:26:49,871
but instead of this wheat crêpe,

2307
01:26:49,938 --> 01:26:52,240
-I would've preferred rice.
-Rice!

2308
01:26:52,307 --> 01:26:53,641
You should've served rice!

2309
01:26:53,708 --> 01:26:58,379
I would've enjoyed it much more,
mixing the sauce with rice.

2310
01:26:58,446 --> 01:27:01,482
-What a shame.
-This fish would taste great with this.

2311
01:27:01,549 --> 01:27:02,684
That'd be amazing.

2312
01:27:02,750 --> 01:27:04,185
They're from the same restaurant.

2313
01:27:04,485 --> 01:27:05,954
THEY SHOULD PROBABLY PARTNER UP

2314
01:27:06,020 --> 01:27:07,655
-The same place?
-Flounder and hot pot rice.

2315
01:27:07,722 --> 01:27:09,324
BUT IT WAS DELICIOUS

2316
01:27:09,390 --> 01:27:12,594
Nam-no is now on a roll
with his fourth consecutive win.

2317
01:27:12,660 --> 01:27:15,563
I can't believe this. Let's hear from him.

2318
01:27:15,630 --> 01:27:18,666
Well, this game
was too advantageous for me.

2319
01:27:21,069 --> 01:27:22,971
I knew I'd win this round.

2320
01:27:23,538 --> 01:27:25,940
Let me be smug. It's my first time
winning four rounds in a row.

2321
01:27:26,007 --> 01:27:27,942
Look at that guy.

2322
01:27:28,009 --> 01:27:29,711
He has won four times in a row,

2323
01:27:29,777 --> 01:27:31,079
but he's still at six wins.

2324
01:27:31,145 --> 01:27:32,981
STILL AT 6 WINS

2325
01:27:33,047 --> 01:27:35,083
He wasn't doing too well before.

2326
01:27:35,149 --> 01:27:37,785
I look forward to seeing
your last-minute effort in the fall.

2327
01:27:37,852 --> 01:27:39,454
-If he wins every week…
-Let's be hopeful.

2328
01:27:39,520 --> 01:27:42,223
Please put a star on Chef Nam-no!

2329
01:27:42,290 --> 01:27:43,725
Right on his chest.

2330
01:27:45,026 --> 01:27:46,894
It's Nam-no's sixth win.

2331
01:27:46,961 --> 01:27:48,296
Congratulations.

2332
01:27:50,632 --> 01:27:52,600
They were the same kind.

2333
01:27:52,900 --> 01:27:54,068
THE 2 INFAMOUS LIGHT EATERS

2334
01:27:54,269 --> 01:27:56,271
WILL WE SEE ANOTHER AWFUL FRIDGE?

2335
01:27:56,504 --> 01:27:59,474
THE CHEFS ARE IN A FRIGHT

2336
01:27:59,540 --> 01:28:00,642
You don't have to worry.

2337
01:28:00,708 --> 01:28:02,443
-I doubt it.
-Are you better than Woo-jae?

2338
01:28:02,677 --> 01:28:04,178
WHAT'S IN COKUN'S FRIDGE?

2339
01:28:04,245 --> 01:28:06,381
-Why is this in here?
-A tape measure?

2340
01:28:06,447 --> 01:28:07,749
Is this a keychain?

2341
01:28:07,815 --> 01:28:10,218
I use it as a drawer
when there's empty space.

2342
01:28:10,285 --> 01:28:12,053
Do you keep sheet music in there?

2343
01:28:12,287 --> 01:28:14,389
AN INTRODUCTION
TO A NEW PARADIGM OF FRIDGE-DRAWER

2344
01:28:14,455 --> 01:28:15,556
I've been eating a lot.

2345
01:28:15,623 --> 01:28:18,726
-How much do you eat?
-How much chicken breast?

2346
01:28:18,793 --> 01:28:19,961
I can eat two packs.

2347
01:28:20,028 --> 01:28:20,862
For a meal?

2348
01:28:20,928 --> 01:28:21,796
No, a day.

2349
01:28:21,863 --> 01:28:22,797
WAIT, WHAT NOW?

2350
01:28:22,864 --> 01:28:24,966
Are you kidding me? Tell me the truth.

2351
01:28:25,033 --> 01:28:27,335
Why do I feel like
I'm at a police station?

2352
01:28:27,402 --> 01:28:28,369
A SPECIAL PRESCRIPTION

2353
01:28:28,569 --> 01:28:29,771
FOR THE LIGHT EATER!

2354
01:28:30,004 --> 01:28:31,406
GOODBYE TO HIS SMALL APPETITE!

2355
01:28:31,673 --> 01:28:33,941
THE BLENDER JAR WON'T COME OFF?

2356
01:28:34,175 --> 01:28:35,810
OFF TO A DIFFICULT START

2357
01:28:36,010 --> 01:28:37,679
IT'S NOT EASY TO FEED THE LIGHT EATER

2358
01:28:37,879 --> 01:28:39,113
HE LOOKS EXTRA-TROUBLED TODAY

2359
01:28:39,314 --> 01:28:42,717
I ALWAYS DANCE WHEN THINGS ARE TOUGH

2360
01:28:42,917 --> 01:28:44,752
POONG-SAN'S DANCE STRATEGY

2361
01:28:44,819 --> 01:28:47,822
No matter how busy you are,
you should at least enjoy a cup of coffee.

2362
01:28:48,022 --> 01:28:50,124
COFFEE TIME WITH GOOD VIBES

2363
01:28:50,291 --> 01:28:52,560
CAN'T GIVE UP ON GOOD-VIBE TECHNIQUES
EVEN WHEN HE'S BUSY

2364
01:28:53,861 --> 01:28:56,731
TODAY, I'M A GUY WITH A BIG APPETITE

2365
01:28:56,798 --> 01:28:58,132
Who called him a light eater?

2366
01:28:58,199 --> 01:28:59,500
-He eats well.
-He eats well.

2367
01:28:59,567 --> 01:29:01,069
I love this place.

2368
01:29:01,135 --> 01:29:03,171
I get compliments just for eating.

2369
01:29:03,738 --> 01:29:05,540
WHICH CHEF UNLOCKED

2370
01:29:05,840 --> 01:29:07,342
CODE KUNST'S APPETITE?

2371
01:29:10,845 --> 01:29:12,880
I could always get a bite

2372
01:29:12,947 --> 01:29:14,816
when I sit next to the judge,

2373
01:29:14,882 --> 01:29:17,051
but he ate everything.

2374
01:29:17,652 --> 01:29:19,954
I never got to use this spoon.

2375
01:29:20,021 --> 01:29:22,223
-You usually could.
-I'm sorry.

2376
01:29:31,666 --> 01:29:36,437
Subtitle translation by: Eunsook Youn
